      "text": "Section 1.482-1(a) establishes the general framework for IRS authority to allocate gross income, deductions, credits, or allowances among controlled taxpayers. (1) Purpose and scope: The primary purpose of section 482 is to ensure that taxpayers clearly reflect income attributable to controlled transactions and to prevent the evasion of taxes with respect to such transactions. The arm's length standard is the cornerstone principle — controlled transactions must be priced as if conducted between uncontrolled parties dealing at arm's length. (2) Authority to make allocations: The Commissioner may distribute, apportion, or allocate gross income, deductions, credits, or allowances between or among organizations, trades, or businesses owned or controlled directly or indirectly by the same interests, if such allocation is necessary to prevent evasion of taxes or to clearly reflect income. This authority applies regardless of whether the entities are incorporated, whether they are organized in the United States or abroad, and whether they are affiliated. (3) Taxpayer's use of section 482: A taxpayer may also apply section 482 principles to its own controlled transactions to ensure that its reported income clearly reflects arm's length results. This includes the ability to reflect arm's length pricing in the taxpayer's original return, subject to the same standards the IRS applies when making allocations.",
