      "title": "Comparability Analysis: Functional Analysis, Contractual Terms, Risk, and Economic Conditions",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-1(i), the comparability of controlled and uncontrolled transactions is evaluated through a multi-factor analysis encompassing: (i) Functional analysis — an examination of the functions performed, assets employed, and risks assumed by each party to the transaction; (ii) Contractual terms — including (A) general principles governing how contractual terms affect comparability, (B) identification of contractual terms whether derived from (1) a written agreement or (2) conduct in the absence of a written agreement, and (C) examples illustrating application; (iii) Risk — including (A) general principles for risk allocation, (B) identification of the party that bears economic risk based on realistic alternatives and actual conduct rather than mere contractual characterization, and (C) illustrative examples; and (iv) Economic conditions — an assessment of market-level factors such as geographic market, market share, competition, and economic cycles that may affect pricing. Together, these factors determine whether controlled transactions are comparable to uncontrolled transactions for purposes of applying the arm's length standard. Contractual terms are derived first from written agreements; where no written agreement exists, terms are inferred from the parties' actual conduct. Risk allocation follows economic substance — the party that controls risk and has the financial capacity to assume it is treated as bearing that risk, regardless of formal contractual assignment.",
