      "title": "Special Circumstances in Controlled Transactions Involving Property or Services",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(v)(4) addresses special circumstances that arise in the context of controlled transactions involving property or services. Under the arm's length standard, the Commissioner may make allocations to reflect the true taxable income of a controlled taxpayer where special circumstances affect the pricing or terms of transactions involving property or services between related parties. These special circumstances may include unique economic conditions, market disruptions, government-imposed restrictions, or other factors that distinguish the controlled transaction from comparable uncontrolled transactions. The determination of an arm's length result must account for these special circumstances to the extent that an uncontrolled taxpayer operating at arm's length would have reflected such circumstances in the pricing, terms, or structure of the transaction. Accordingly, the analysis of comparability must be adjusted to account for any material differences arising from these special circumstances, consistent with the comparability standards set forth under §1.482-1(d). Where reliable adjustments cannot be made to account for such differences, the comparability of a potential uncontrolled transaction may be diminished or eliminated. The provision underscores that all relevant facts and circumstances must be considered in determining whether a controlled transaction satisfies the arm's length standard, including circumstances that are unique or transitory in nature.",
