      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(e)",
      "title": "Arm's Length Range — Determination and Application",
      "text": "§ 1.482-1(e) establishes the arm's length range concept for evaluating controlled transactions. (1) In general: A single arm's length price or result is the benchmark for controlled transactions; however, because transfer pricing is not an exact science, a range of acceptable results may exist. Where the results of a controlled transaction fall within the arm's length range, no allocation will be made. Where the results fall outside the arm's length range, the Commissioner may adjust the controlled party's results to the most appropriate point within the range — generally the median — unless the taxpayer demonstrates that a different point within the range is more appropriate. (2) Determination of arm's length range: The arm's length range is derived from the application of a single transfer pricing method to two or more uncontrolled comparables. When the comparables are not sufficiently similar to warrant equal weight, a statistical measure such as the interquartile range may be used to identify the most reliable portion of the full range. Results within this narrowed range are considered arm's length. The range concept aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter III, which similarly permits a range of arm's length figures where comparability is imperfect. The interquartile range is the most commonly applied statistical tool to identify the acceptable range, though other measures may be appropriate where justified. The provision reinforces that comparability analysis — including functional analysis, contractual terms, economic circumstances, and business strategies — underpins the construction of any valid arm's length range.",
