      "id": "irc482-1i",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "Arm's Length Range Construction and Adjustment Methodology under § 1.482-1(i)",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-1(i), the arm's length range is established by applying a single transfer pricing method to two or more uncontrolled comparables. The selection of comparables must reflect transactions or entities that are sufficiently similar to the controlled transaction to provide a reliable measure of an arm's length result. Where the comparables are not equally reliable, the range may be adjusted—most commonly by applying the interquartile range—to increase reliability by excluding the least reliable data points from the outer bounds of the full range. The interquartile range spans from the 25th to the 75th percentile of results derived from the selected comparables. If the taxpayer's reported results fall within the arm's length range (as potentially narrowed), no allocation is required. If the taxpayer's results fall outside the arm's length range, the Commissioner may make an allocation to the median of the range unless the taxpayer establishes that a different point within the range is more appropriate. The existence of an arm's length range is not a prerequisite for the Commissioner to make an allocation; an allocation may be made even where only a single comparable is available or where the range cannot be reliably established. These principles align with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter III, which similarly addresses the construction of arm's length ranges and the use of statistical tools such as the interquartile range to improve comparability and reliability.",
