      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(f)",
      "title": "Scope of Review — General Principles for Section 482 Allocations",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(f)(1) establishes the general scope of the Commissioner's authority to review and make allocations under Section 482. The Commissioner may distribute, apportion, or allocate gross income, deductions, credits, or allowances between or among controlled taxpayers if such allocation is necessary to prevent evasion of taxes or to clearly reflect the income of any controlled taxpayer. The scope of review encompasses all transactions between or among members of a controlled group, including arrangements relating to tangible property, intangible property, services, loans, and cost sharing arrangements. The review is not limited to a single transaction or taxable year but may encompass a pattern of transactions, contractual arrangements, and the overall economic substance of intercompany dealings. The arm's length standard, as the governing principle, requires that the results of controlled transactions be consistent with the results that would have been realized had uncontrolled taxpayers engaged in the same transactions under the same circumstances. The Commissioner's authority under this scope extends to examining whether the form of a transaction reflects its substance, whether contractual terms are consistent with economic reality, and whether the allocation of risks and functions among controlled parties corresponds to actual conduct. This provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I, which similarly affirms the breadth of tax authority review over related-party transactions to ensure income is accurately reflected.",
