      "title": "Comparability Analysis Adjustments: Aggregation, Actual Transactions, Multiple Year Data, and Statistical Techniques",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(i) addresses the analytical framework for applying transfer pricing methods with greater accuracy and reliability. (i)(A) Aggregation: Transactions may be aggregated if the economic substance of the transactions is closely interlinked such that they cannot be evaluated adequately on a separate basis. (i)(B) provides illustrative examples. (ii)(A) Allocation based on taxpayer's actual transactions: The district director will determine the arm's length result based on the taxpayer's actual transactions and will not restructure those transactions except in rare circumstances where the economic substance differs from the form. (iii)(A) Multiple year data: In general, the arm's length result is determined using data from the taxable year under review. However, multiple year data may be considered where it is relevant to understanding the long-term conduct of the parties and the pricing of comparable transactions. (iii)(B) Circumstances: Multiple year data may be appropriate where business cycles or product life cycles affect comparability. (iii)(C) Comparable effect over comparable period: Any multiple year analysis must be applied consistently to both the controlled and uncontrolled transactions. (iii)(D) Applications of methods: Where applicable, interquartile ranges and averages derived from multiple year data may improve reliability. (iii)(E) provides illustrative examples. (iv) Product lines and statistical techniques: The reliability of a transfer pricing analysis may be enhanced by applying results across product lines and by employing statistical techniques such as regression analysis or the interquartile range to identify arm's length results.",
