      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(v)",
      "title": "IRC §482 Allocations Apply to Results, Not Methods — §1.482-1(v)",
      "text": "Under §1.482-1(v), the Commissioner's authority to make allocations among controlled entities applies to the results of transactions, not to the methods used by the taxpayer. This means that even if a taxpayer employs a transfer pricing method that is otherwise acceptable or reasonable, the Commissioner may nonetheless make an allocation if the results of applying that method do not reflect an arm's length outcome. The inquiry focuses on whether the controlled transaction produces a result consistent with what uncontrolled parties would have achieved under comparable circumstances, rather than on whether the taxpayer followed any particular methodology. A method that is technically applied but yields a non-arm's-length result remains subject to allocation. This principle reinforces the arm's length standard as the overarching benchmark: procedural or methodological compliance does not insulate a taxpayer from adjustment if the economic outcome diverges from arm's length. The provision is illustrated by an example demonstrating that the Commissioner may adjust taxable income to reflect the correct arm's length result regardless of which pricing method the taxpayer nominally employed. This approach aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I, which similarly emphasizes that the arm's length principle is an outcomes-based standard and that the selection of a method is a means to achieving an arm's length result, not an end in itself.",
