      "title": "Collateral Adjustments and Correlative Allocations Under Section 482",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(g) addresses collateral adjustments that must be made with respect to allocations under Section 482. Where the IRS makes a primary allocation (reallocation of income or deductions between controlled taxpayers), corresponding correlative adjustments must be made to the accounts of the other affected controlled party to prevent double taxation or distortion of income. Specifically, if an allocation is made to one member of a controlled group, a correlative allocation is required for the other member(s) involved in the transaction so that the combined tax treatment reflects the arm's length result on both sides of the transaction. These collateral adjustments are integral to ensuring that a Section 482 reallocation does not produce an economically inconsistent or duplicative result across the controlled group. The correlative allocation provisions align with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines' concept of corresponding adjustments (Chapter IV), which similarly require that where one jurisdiction adjusts a taxpayer's profits upward, the other jurisdiction involved should make a corresponding downward adjustment to avoid economic double taxation. Under US domestic rules, the correlative allocation mechanism operates to preserve the integrity of the arm's length standard by ensuring symmetry between primary and secondary adjustments within a controlled group.",
