      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "Arm's Length Standard — General Provisions and Setoff Rule (§ 1.482-1(i))",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(i) establishes general principles governing the application of the arm's length standard under IRC Section 482. The arm's length standard requires that the results of a controlled transaction be consistent with the results that would have been realized if uncontrolled taxpayers had engaged in the same transaction under the same circumstances. When the IRS makes an allocation under Section 482, the taxpayer may assert a setoff against that allocation for any correlative transaction or item that would have been taken into account in determining the arm's length result for the transaction being adjusted. A setoff is permitted only if the taxpayer establishes that: (a) the transaction that is the basis of the setoff was not at arm's length; (b) the proposed setoff is directly related to the adjusted transaction; and (c) the taxpayer establishes the amount of the setoff with reasonable specificity. The setoff rule is intended to prevent the IRS from making one-sided adjustments that overstate taxable income by ignoring related transactions that, when considered together, produce an overall arm's length result. Any setoff claimed by the taxpayer must be clearly identified and substantiated at the time the taxpayer proposes the setoff, generally during the examination or before the Tax Court. The provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines concepts of overall arm's length outcomes and the principle that interrelated transactions should be evaluated in context.",
