      "title": "Special Rules: Effect of Foreign Legal Restrictions on Arm's Length Determinations",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(h) establishes special rules applicable to transfer pricing determinations under IRC Section 482. Subsection (h)(1) reserves a small taxpayer safe harbor, indicating that relief provisions for smaller taxpayers have been contemplated but not yet promulgated. Subsection (h)(2) addresses the effect of foreign legal restrictions on the arm's length analysis, recognizing that certain cross-border transactions may be subject to foreign laws, regulations, or governmental restrictions that constrain the prices or terms a controlled taxpayer may charge or receive. Where bona fide foreign legal restrictions exist and demonstrably limit the controlled taxpayer's ability to transact at arm's length prices, those restrictions may be taken into account in determining the arm's length result. The taxpayer bears the burden of establishing that the foreign legal restriction is legally binding, applies specifically to the transaction at issue, and that uncontrolled parties operating under comparable restrictions would have structured their transactions similarly. The provision ensures that transfer pricing outcomes reflect economic reality as constrained by applicable foreign law, while preventing taxpayers from using purported foreign restrictions as a pretext for artificial pricing arrangements that would not withstand arm's length scrutiny. This approach is broadly consistent with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I guidance on the relevance of government-imposed restrictions to the arm's length principle.",
