      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "Section 482 Transfer Pricing — Core Definitions",
      "text": "§ 1.482-1(i) sets forth the definitions applicable throughout the Section 482 transfer pricing regulations. These definitions establish the foundational terminology governing controlled transactions between related parties, including key concepts such as 'controlled,' 'controlled taxpayer,' 'uncontrolled,' 'uncontrolled taxpayer,' 'controlled transaction,' 'uncontrolled transaction,' 'arm's length result,' 'arm's length range,' 'median,' 'interquartile range,' 'taxpayer,' and 'tax.' A 'controlled' transaction or taxpayer refers to any transaction between or involving two or more members of the same group of controlled entities. An 'uncontrolled' transaction or taxpayer refers to transactions or parties that are not under common control. The 'arm's length result' is the amount of consideration that would have been charged or paid in a controlled transaction if the same transaction had been entered into between uncontrolled taxpayers dealing at arm's length under the same or similar circumstances. The 'arm's length range' encompasses a range of arm's length results derived from two or more uncontrolled transactions of the same type that can provide comparable data for purposes of establishing an arm's length price or allocation. These definitions underpin the comparability framework and method-selection analyses required under the Section 482 regulations, and align broadly with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines' definitions of 'associated enterprises,' 'controlled transactions,' and the 'arm's length principle' as articulated in Chapter I.",
