      "title": "Arm's Length Interest Rate and Period for Intercompany Indebtedness under § 1.482-2(a)",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(i) and the associated § 1.482-2(a) provisions govern the imputation and determination of arm's length interest on intercompany loans and indebtedness between controlled entities. The rules address: (i) the general requirement that interest be charged on bona fide indebtedness between controlled parties at an arm's length rate; (ii) the distinction between bona fide indebtedness and alleged indebtedness that may be recharacterized; (iii) the period for which interest shall be charged, subject to exceptions for (A) intercompany transactions arising in the ordinary course of business, (B) trade or business of the debtor member located outside the United States, (C) regular trade practice of the creditor member or others in the creditor's industry, and (D) property purchased for resale in a foreign country, including rules for the interest-free period, average collection period, and illustrative examples; and (iv) payment and book entry requirements. The arm's length interest rate standard requires that the rate charged on intercompany debt reflect what unrelated parties would negotiate under comparable circumstances, consistent with the comparable uncontrolled transaction (CUT) method applied to financial transactions. The IRS may allocate income, deductions, or credits to reflect an arm's length result where no interest, or a non-arm's length rate of interest, has been charged on controlled indebtedness. These rules align broadly with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter X on financial transactions.",
