      "title": "Safe Haven Interest Rates for Intercompany Loans and Advances — § 1.482-1(i)",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(i) establishes the framework for determining arm's length interest rates on intercompany loans and advances between controlled parties. The provision addresses: (i) general principles for charging interest on intercompany financing arrangements; (ii) the rule that funds are treated as obtained at the situs of the borrower for sourcing and pricing purposes; (iii) safe haven interest rates for loans and advances made after May 8, 1986, including: (A) applicability rules comprising a general rule and a grandfather rule for existing loans, (B) a safe haven interest rate range based on the Applicable Federal Rate (AFR), (C) the definition and determination of the Applicable Federal Rate, (D) special treatment where the lender is in the business of making loans, and (E) rules applicable to foreign currency loans. The provision further addresses (3) coordination requirements where interest adjustments are also required under other Internal Revenue Code sections, ensuring that § 482 interest allocations are harmonized with other Code provisions (e.g., OID and below-market loan rules), and (4) illustrative examples. The safe haven range generally permits interest rates between 100% and 130% of the AFR for the relevant term, providing a corridor within which controlled parties may set intercompany loan rates without triggering an IRS reallocation. Loans outside this corridor must be supported by arm's length evidence. Foreign currency loans require application of appropriate market rates for the relevant currency. The provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter X (Financial Transactions) principles regarding comparability of loan terms, creditworthiness, and market benchmarking.",
