      "title": "Intercompany Services — Arm's Length Charge and Benefit Test Framework",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(b) establishes the framework governing the allocation of income and deductions arising from performance of services by one controlled entity for another. The provision sets out: (1) a general rule requiring that intercompany services be priced consistent with the arm's length standard; (2) a benefit test to determine whether a service has been rendered — i.e., whether the activity provides an anticipated economic or commercial value to the recipient that an uncontrolled party would be willing to pay for or perform for itself; (3) the arm's length charge that must be determined for compensable services; (4) the costs or deductions that must be taken into account in computing the charge; (5) costs and deductions that are excluded from the computation; (6) the methods available to determine an arm's length price for services; (7) rules for certain categories of services; and (8) rules addressing services rendered in connection with property transfers. Collectively, these sub-provisions implement the arm's length principle for intercompany service arrangements and align with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter VII, which similarly applies a benefit test and requires that intragroup services be charged at an arm's length price where a comparable uncontrolled transaction exists.",
