      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(c)",
      "title": "Arm's Length Standard for Use of Tangible Property Between Controlled Parties",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(c) establishes the general rule governing transactions involving the use of tangible property between controlled parties. Under this provision, where one member of a controlled group makes tangible property available for use by another member, the arm's length charge for such use must reflect the amount that an uncontrolled party would have charged under the same or similar circumstances. The arm's length charge is determined by reference to the amount a comparable uncontrolled party would charge for the use of comparable property under comparable conditions, taking into account the nature of the property, the duration of use, the geographic market, risks assumed, and the relative bargaining positions of the parties. This provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I principles regarding the arm's length principle as applied to property usage arrangements, including leases, licenses of tangible assets, and similar arrangements. Controlled parties must ensure that intercompany charges for the use of tangible property are consistent with what uncontrolled parties would negotiate in an open market, and must maintain contemporaneous documentation sufficient to demonstrate that the pricing methodology applied satisfies the arm's length standard.",
