      "title": "Safe Haven Rental Charge for Leased Property Between Controlled Parties",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(i) addresses the arm's length standard as applied to rental arrangements between controlled parties. The provision establishes general principles governing intercompany lease transactions and introduces a safe haven rental charge mechanism that permits controlled taxpayers to use a specified rental rate without triggering reallocation by the IRS, provided the charge falls within the prescribed range. The safe haven rental charge provides a simplified compliance option for intercompany property leasing, reducing documentation burden and audit risk when the stipulated rate is applied. The sublease rules extend these principles to tiered leasing arrangements, where a controlled party acts as an intermediate lessee and subleases the property to another related party. In such cases, the arm's length rental charge for the sublease must be consistent with the rates that unrelated parties would negotiate in comparable circumstances, taking into account the cost of the head lease, market rental rates, and any value added by the intermediate lessee. The provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter VI and general comparable uncontrolled price principles by requiring that intercompany rental charges reflect what independent enterprises would agree to under similar conditions. Controlled taxpayers utilizing the safe haven are relieved from the obligation to perform a full comparability analysis for the rental charge, provided they document their election and comply with the applicable rate parameters. Failure to apply an arm's length rental charge, or to satisfy safe haven conditions, may result in IRS reallocation of income or deductions under the general arm's length standard of § 1.482-1.",
