      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(a)",
      "title": "Section 482 General Authority — Arm's Length Standard for Controlled Transactions",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(a) establishes the IRS Commissioner's authority to allocate gross income, deductions, credits, or allowances between or among two or more organizations, trades, or businesses owned or controlled directly or indirectly by the same interests. The purpose of this authority is to prevent evasion of taxes or to clearly reflect the income of any such organizations, trades, or businesses. The standard applied is the arm's length standard: the amount charged in a controlled transaction must be consistent with the amount that would have been charged in a comparable transaction between uncontrolled parties under comparable circumstances. This general provision serves as the foundational authority for all transfer pricing allocations under Section 482, underpinning the specific methods and rules set forth in the subsequent subsections and related regulations. The arm's length standard aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Article 9 standard applicable to associated enterprises, reflecting the internationally accepted benchmark for evaluating intercompany pricing. Controlled transactions subject to this authority include transfers of tangible property, intangible property, services, loans, and cost sharing arrangements. The Commissioner may make allocations regardless of whether the controlled parties have filed a tax return, and such allocations are intended to place controlled taxpayers on a tax parity with uncontrolled taxpayers engaging in the same or similar transactions.",
