      "text": "Section 1.482-1(b) establishes the foundational framework governing the arm's length standard applicable to controlled transactions. The Commissioner may allocate gross income, deductions, credits, or allowances between or among two or more organizations, trades, or businesses owned or controlled by the same interests if such allocation is necessary to prevent the evasion of taxes or to clearly reflect the income of any controlled taxpayer. The arm's length result of a controlled transaction is determined by reference to the results of comparable transactions between uncontrolled parties under comparable circumstances. Comparability is assessed by examining the functions performed, risks assumed, and contractual terms of the transactions, as well as the economic conditions and the nature of the property or services transferred. The best method rule requires that the method selected must provide the most reliable measure of an arm's length result. Reliability is determined by the degree of comparability between the controlled transaction and any uncontrolled comparables, and the quality and completeness of the data and assumptions used in the analysis. Where a range of arm's length results exists, any result within the arm's length range will generally be considered arm's length. If the controlled transaction falls outside the arm's length range, an allocation may be made to the median of the range. This provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I (arm's length principle) and Chapter III (comparability analysis).",
