      "title": "Arm's Length Standard: Comparability, Data, and Arm's Length Range Framework",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-1(i), the arm's length standard requires that the results of a controlled transaction be consistent with results that would have been realized by uncontrolled taxpayers engaged in the same transaction under the same circumstances. Key components include: (i) general application of the arm's length standard; (ii) comparability analysis, which requires (A) a general assessment of whether controlled and uncontrolled transactions are comparable, and (B) appropriate adjustments for material differences between controlled and uncontrolled transactions that would affect the price or profit measure used; (iii) the quality of data and assumptions underlying the analysis, including the reliability and completeness of comparability data; (3) the arm's length range, which represents the range of acceptable arm's length results derived from two or more uncontrolled comparables that are sufficiently similar to the controlled transaction, within which the controlled transaction price or profit must fall to avoid adjustment; (4) illustrative examples demonstrating application of these principles; and (5) indirect evidence of comparable uncontrolled transactions, which may be used where direct comparables are unavailable. Adjustments must be made for differences in contractual terms, risk allocation, economic conditions, property or services involved, and business functions that materially affect the comparability of the transactions. The arm's length range is typically established using the interquartile range or other statistical method when the full range is not reliable. If the controlled transaction result falls outside the arm's length range, the Commissioner may adjust the result to the median or other appropriate point within the range.",
