      "title": "Resale Price Method — Arm's Length Price Determination Under IRC § 482",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(c) establishes the resale price method (RPM) as an approach for determining arm's length prices in controlled transactions. Under the general rule, the resale price method evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction is arm's length by reference to the gross profit margin realized by a comparable uncontrolled reseller. The arm's length price is determined by reducing the applicable resale price by an appropriate gross profit margin, reflecting the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed by the reseller. The gross profit margin used must be derived from comparable uncontrolled transactions, taking into account factors such as the similarity of products, contractual terms, economic conditions, and the level of the market at which the reseller operates. Where the reseller performs significant value-adding functions beyond mere distribution — such as product modification, marketing intangible development, or assumption of inventory risk — adjustments to the gross profit margin may be required to achieve comparability. This method is most reliably applied when the reseller does not add substantial value to the product prior to resale and when close functional and risk comparables are available. The resale price method aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the RPM as one of the traditional transaction methods.",
