      "title": "Resale Price Method — General Framework and Comparability Considerations",
      "text": "The resale price method evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction is arm's length by reference to the gross profit margin realized in comparable uncontrolled transactions. The applicable resale price is the price at which property purchased in a controlled transaction is resold in an uncontrolled transaction. The appropriate gross profit is derived from the gross profit margin earned by the reseller in comparable uncontrolled purchases and resales, or by uncontrolled resellers in comparable transactions. This margin reflects the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed by the reseller. The arm's length range is established from the results of multiple comparable uncontrolled transactions after applying the resale price method. Comparability and reliability considerations require that the resale price method produces the most reliable measure of an arm's length result when: the reseller does not add substantial value to the property prior to resale; there is a short elapsed time between the controlled purchase and the uncontrolled resale; and the reseller performs similar functions, assumes similar risks, and uses similar resources in the controlled and uncontrolled transactions. Differences in accounting practices, product characteristics, and contractual terms that materially affect gross profit margins must be accounted for through adjustments. The reliability of this method is generally greater when comparables are internal (i.e., the same reseller's uncontrolled transactions) rather than external, and when the reseller does not hold significant intangible property that contributes to the resale price.",
