      "text": "Under § 1.482-1(i), the comparability analysis framework establishes the standards for evaluating whether controlled and uncontrolled transactions are sufficiently comparable to support a transfer pricing determination. The provision addresses: (i) the general comparability standard; (ii) comparability factors including (A) functional comparability — requiring analysis of functions performed, assets employed, and risks assumed by each party; (B) other comparability factors such as contractual terms, economic conditions, and product or service characteristics; (C) adjustments for differences between controlled and uncontrolled transactions — permitting or requiring quantitative adjustments to eliminate the effect of material differences that would affect the price or profit metric being compared; and (D) sales agent treatment for characterizing the nature of a controlled party's role in distribution arrangements. The provision further governs (iii) data and assumptions, including (A) the general standard for selecting and applying data used in the comparability analysis, and (B) consistency in accounting, requiring that financial data used across controlled and uncontrolled transactions be presented on a consistent accounting basis to ensure valid comparisons. These principles align with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I comparability standards, particularly the five comparability factors (functions, risks, contractual terms, economic circumstances, and business strategies). The provision concludes with (4) illustrative examples demonstrating application of the comparability framework.",
