      "title": "Cost Plus Method — Determination of Arm's Length Price Under § 1.482-1(d)",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(d) addresses the cost plus method as a means of determining an arm's length price between controlled taxpayers. Under the general rule, the cost plus method evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction is arm's length by reference to the gross profit markup earned by comparable uncontrolled taxpayers in comparable transactions. The method begins with the costs incurred by the supplier of property or services in a controlled transaction, then adds an appropriate gross profit markup. The resulting amount, after adding the costs incurred, is treated as the arm's length price. The gross profit markup is derived from comparable uncontrolled transactions and must reflect functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed, consistent with the comparability standards applicable under § 1.482. Comparability under the cost plus method is particularly sensitive to consistency in accounting methods used to determine costs, and adjustments may be required where differences in cost accounting practices affect the reliability of the comparison. The method aligns with the cost plus method described in Chapter II of the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines, which similarly focuses on the supplier's costs as the starting point and applies a markup reflective of market conditions. The determination of the arm's length price under this method requires identification of appropriate cost bases and markup rates drawn from uncontrolled comparables, and the reliability of the method depends on the degree of comparability between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions.",
