      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "Resale Price Method — General Framework, Appropriate Gross Profit, and Arm's Length Range",
      "text": "The resale price method evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction is arm's length by reference to the gross profit margin realized in comparable uncontrolled transactions. Under § 1.482-1(i), the method operates as follows: (i) In general — the resale price method measures the value of functions performed by a controlled reseller by reference to the gross profit margin earned by comparable uncontrolled distributors performing similar functions under similar circumstances; (ii) Appropriate gross profit — the appropriate gross profit is determined by multiplying the applicable resale price by a gross profit percentage derived from comparable uncontrolled transactions, with the resulting gross profit subtracted from the applicable resale price to determine the arm's length purchase price from the controlled supplier; (iii) Arm's length range — an arm's length range is established from the results of two or more comparable uncontrolled transactions, with the range potentially subject to statistical narrowing (e.g., interquartile range) where the number of comparables supports such refinement; (3) Comparability and reliability considerations — the reliability of the resale price method depends significantly on the similarity of functions performed, risks assumed, and contractual terms between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions, with gross profit margins being particularly sensitive to functional differences. Product similarity is less critical than under the comparable uncontrolled price method, but functional comparability — including inventory risk, warranty obligations, and sales and marketing activities — is paramount. The method aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, Part B, Section B on the resale price method.",
