      "title": "Comparability Analysis Framework: Functional Comparability, Adjustments, and Data Consistency",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(i) establishes the analytical framework for determining comparability between controlled and uncontrolled transactions. Under (i)(ii), comparability is assessed through: (A) Functional comparability — requiring analysis of the functions performed, risks assumed, and assets employed by each party, consistent with a functional analysis approach; (B) Other comparability factors — including contractual terms, economic conditions, and property or services involved; (C) Adjustments for differences — where material differences exist between controlled and uncontrolled transactions, reasonably accurate adjustments must be made to eliminate the effect of such differences on price or profit; and (D) Purchasing agent — addressing circumstances where a controlled party acts in the capacity of a purchasing agent rather than a principal, which affects the appropriate method and comparability standard. Under (i)(iii), data and assumptions used in the analysis must be reliable and consistent: (A) In general, the best available data must be used, and assumptions must be reasonable given the facts and circumstances; (B) Consistency in accounting requires that differences in accounting practices between controlled and uncontrolled transactions be identified and adjusted to ensure that the data used in the comparability analysis reflects economically equivalent measures. The provision closes with (i)(4) Examples illustrating application of these comparability principles. This framework aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapters I and III, which similarly require functional analysis and comparability adjustments as foundational steps in any arm's length analysis.",
