      "title": "Unspecified Methods — General Rule for Transfer Pricing",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(e) provides that, in determining the true taxable income of a controlled taxpayer, the Commissioner may use any method not specified in the section 482 regulations if the method provides the most reliable measure of an arm's length result under the facts and circumstances. The regulations under section 482 specify methods for determining arm's length results for various types of controlled transactions. However, these enumerated methods are not exhaustive, and unspecified methods may be used if they are more reliable than the specified methods. The best method rule under § 1.482-1(c) applies equally to unspecified methods: the method selected must provide the most reliable measure of an arm's length result, taking into account the degree of comparability between the controlled transaction and uncontrolled comparables, and the quality of the data and assumptions used in the analysis. An unspecified method should be applied in accordance with the principles of the best method rule, and taxpayers must document why an unspecified method provides a more reliable measure of an arm's length result than any specified method. The example provided in paragraph (e)(2) illustrates application of an unspecified method where the standard specified methods do not reliably measure the arm's length result for a particular controlled transaction, demonstrating that flexibility exists within the regulatory framework to accommodate unique or complex intercompany arrangements not fully addressed by enumerated methods.",
