      "id": "irc482-1c",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(c)",
      "title": "Comparable Uncontrolled Transaction (CUT) Method — General Framework and Comparability Standards",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(c) establishes the Comparable Uncontrolled Transaction (CUT) method as a mechanism for evaluating whether controlled transactions reflect arm's length conditions by reference to uncontrolled transactions between unrelated parties. Under the general rule, the CUT method measures the arm's length result by identifying comparable uncontrolled transactions that are sufficiently similar to the controlled transaction under review. Comparability and reliability considerations require that the uncontrolled transaction be comparable in terms of the functions performed, risks assumed, contractual terms, economic conditions, and property or services transferred. Where differences exist between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions, adjustments must be made if those differences have a material effect on the price, and the reliability of the CUT method depends on the degree of comparability between the transactions and the availability and quality of data used to make any required adjustments. Factors affecting comparability include product similarity, contractual terms, geographic market conditions, and the level of the market at which the transactions occur. The CUT method is generally considered the most reliable measure of an arm's length result when highly comparable uncontrolled transactions can be identified. This provision aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the Comparable Uncontrolled Price (CUP) method, which similarly prioritizes direct transactional comparability as the highest-order benchmark for establishing arm's length pricing.",
