      "id": "irc482-1i",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "Best Method Rule — Reliability, Comparability, and Arm's Length Range Standards for Transfer Pricing Methods",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-1(i), the arm's length standard is applied by determining the results that would have been realized if uncontrolled taxpayers had engaged in the same transaction under the same circumstances. The provision establishes the analytical framework for selecting and applying transfer pricing methods, requiring that the method selected provide the most reliable measure of an arm's length result.\n\nReliability: The reliability of a method depends on the degree of comparability between the controlled transaction and uncontrolled comparables, and on the completeness and accuracy of the underlying data. A method that requires fewer adjustments for comparability differences is generally more reliable.\n\nComparability — In General: Uncontrolled transactions are comparable to controlled transactions if they are sufficiently similar that they provide a reliable measure of an arm's length result. Factors affecting comparability include: (1) for intangible property, the comparable intangible must be used in connection with similar products or processes and under similar circumstances, considering the profit potential of the intangible, its stage of development, rights to receive updates, exclusivity, and duration; (2) comparable circumstances include contractual terms, economic conditions, functions performed, risks assumed, and assets employed.\n\nData and Assumptions: The reliability of a pricing method depends on the quality of available data and the reasonableness of assumptions required. Where data are incomplete or assumptions speculative, reliability is reduced.\n\nArm's Length Range: Where two or more uncontrolled transactions meet comparability standards, the arm's length result may be a range of values rather than a single point. If the controlled transaction falls within the arm's length range, no allocation is required. If outside the range, the IRS will generally adjust to the median of the range. The interquartile range is a common statistical method for defining the arm's length range, consistent with OECD Guidelines Chapter III guidance on arm's length ranges.",
