      "title": "Unspecified Transfer Pricing Methods — General Rule",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(d) establishes that the methods specified elsewhere in the section 482 regulations are not exclusive. Where none of the specified methods set out in the regulations (e.g., those governing transfers of tangible property, intangible property, services, or loans) provide the most reliable measure of an arm's length result, an unspecified method may be used. Any unspecified method must be applied in accordance with the best method rule under § 1.482-1(c), meaning that the method selected must provide the most reliable measure of an arm's length result based on the relevant facts and circumstances. The comparability and reliability factors that apply to specified methods apply equally to unspecified methods. The taxpayer must demonstrate that the unspecified method produces a result that is consistent with an arm's length result and must document the rationale for concluding that no specified method provides a more reliable measure. An example illustrates the application of an unspecified method where specified methods do not adequately capture the economic substance of the controlled transaction under review.",
