      "title": "Special Rules for Transfers of Intangible Property — Form of Consideration and Periodic Adjustments",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(f) establishes special rules applicable to transfers of intangible property between controlled entities. Paragraph (f)(1) addresses the form of consideration required in intangible property transfers, providing that the arm's length amount of consideration for an intangible transfer must reflect the form of payment that uncontrolled parties would use under comparable circumstances, including lump-sum payments, royalties, or other compensation structures. Paragraph (f)(2) addresses periodic adjustments, providing that the IRS may make periodic adjustments to the consideration charged for an intangible transfer in subsequent years if the actual results of the arrangement differ materially from the results projected at the time of the transfer. This periodic adjustment mechanism — commonly referred to as the 'super-royalty' provision — reflects the principle that the arm's length consideration for high-profit-potential intangibles must be commensurate with the income attributable to the intangible over time. These provisions operate in conjunction with the commensurate-with-income standard under IRC § 482 and align with OECD Guidelines Chapter VI principles regarding the valuation of intangibles and the use of price adjustment mechanisms where ex ante valuations prove unreliable. The periodic adjustment rules are designed to prevent controlled taxpayers from underpricing intangible transfers by locking in artificially low royalty rates or lump-sum amounts at the time of transfer, particularly where the intangible subsequently generates income significantly exceeding initial projections.",
