      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "Intangible Property Ownership Identification and Arm's Length Consideration under IRC §482",
      "text": "Under §1.482-1(i), the arm's length consideration for transfers involving intangible property must reflect the full value of the intangible, and consideration may not be artificially limited. Ownership of intangible property is determined by reference to legal protection where available (e.g., patents, trademarks, copyrights held by a legally recognized owner), and by economic ownership principles where legal protection does not apply. For intangible property that is not legally protected, ownership is attributed to the controlled taxpayer that developed, maintained, or otherwise holds the economic rights to exploit the intangible. Where one controlled party provides assistance (e.g., services, funding, know-how) to the owner of an intangible, allocations may be made to ensure that the assisting party is compensated at arm's length for such assistance. The consideration for intangible transfers is not artificially capped or limited by the form of the transaction or contractual terms that do not reflect arm's length outcomes. Lump sum payments for intangible property transfers must also reflect an arm's length amount, determined by reference to the present value of anticipated benefits the transferee would derive from the intangible over its useful life. These provisions align with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter VI regarding the identification of the legal and economic owner of intangibles, and the requirement that all functions performed, assets used, and risks assumed in developing, enhancing, maintaining, protecting, and exploiting (DEMPE) intangibles be appropriately compensated.",
