      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "Comparable Profits Method — General Framework and Applicability",
      "text": "Section 1.482-5 establishes the Comparable Profits Method (CPM) as a transfer pricing method for determining arm's length results in controlled transactions. Under CPM, the arm's length result is determined by reference to the amount of operating profit that uncontrolled taxpayers earn from comparable transactions, measured by profit level indicators (PLIs) such as the ratio of operating profit to sales, operating assets, or total costs. The method identifies comparable uncontrolled companies performing similar functions, bearing similar risks, and employing similar assets in comparable markets. A tested party is selected — generally the party to the controlled transaction for which a reliable set of data can be identified — and its reported operating profit is compared against the range of operating profits earned by comparable uncontrolled companies. General exceptions apply where application of the CPM is inappropriate due to significant differences in the facts and circumstances that cannot be adequately accounted for. The CPM aligns broadly with the OECD Guidelines' Transactional Net Margin Method (TNMM) as articulated in Chapter II, Part III of the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines, in its reliance on net profit indicators derived from comparable uncontrolled parties. The method is particularly useful when one party to the controlled transaction performs routine functions and bears limited risk, making it the appropriate tested party for benchmarking purposes.",
