      "id": "irc482-1a",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(a)",
      "title": "Section 482 General Authority — Arm's Length Standard for Controlled Transactions",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(a) establishes the general authority of the IRS to allocate gross income, deductions, credits, or allowances between or among two or more organizations, trades, or businesses owned or controlled directly or indirectly by the same interests. The purpose of section 482 is to ensure that taxpayers clearly reflect income attributable to controlled transactions and to prevent the avoidance of taxes with respect to such transactions. The standard to be applied in every case is that of a taxpayer dealing at arm's length with an uncontrolled taxpayer. A controlled transaction meets the arm's length standard if the results of the transaction are consistent with the results that would have been realized if uncontrolled taxpayers had engaged in the same transaction under the same circumstances. In evaluating whether a controlled transaction satisfies the arm's length standard, the IRS will consider the facts and circumstances of each case, including the functions performed, risks assumed, and contractual terms agreed upon by the parties. The arm's length standard is the foundational principle underlying all transfer pricing determinations under the Internal Revenue Code, and it is consistent with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines' arm's length principle as articulated in Article 9 of the OECD Model Tax Convention.",
