      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(b)",
      "title": "Arm's Length Standard — General Rule and Tested Party Selection",
      "text": "§ 1.482-1(b) establishes the foundational arm's length standard governing all controlled transactions. (1) In general: The arm's length result of a controlled transaction must be determined by reference to the results of comparable transactions between uncontrolled taxpayers under comparable circumstances. The standard requires that the amount charged in any intercompany transaction be consistent with what would have been charged between unrelated parties dealing at arm's length. The Commissioner may make allocations between or among members of a controlled group to clearly reflect income if the prices charged do not reflect arm's length results. (2) Tested party: The tested party is the participant in the controlled transaction whose operating results, when compared to the results of comparable uncontrolled transactions or companies, provide the most reliable measure of an arm's length result. The tested party is typically the party to which a transfer pricing method can be applied in the most reliable manner and for which the most reliable data are available. Generally, the tested party will be the less complex entity — the one lacking valuable intangibles, unique assets, or bearing limited risks — while the more complex entity is benchmarked indirectly through the tested party's results. This selection principle aligns with OECD Guidelines Chapter I (paragraphs 1.6–1.10) on the arm's length principle and Chapter III on comparability analysis, including guidance on selection of the tested party in one-sided methods.",
