      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "§ 1.482-1(i): Definitions — Tested Party, Arm's Length Range, and Profit Level Indicators",
      "text": "§ 1.482-1(i) sets forth foundational definitions governing the application of the arm's length standard under Section 482. (i) In general: Provides definitions applicable to the transfer pricing rules broadly. (ii) Adjustments for tested party: Defines the 'tested party' as the controlled taxpayer whose operating results are compared to those of uncontrolled taxpayers in applying a transfer pricing method; adjustments are made to the tested party's results to achieve arm's length pricing. The tested party is generally the entity to which a transfer pricing method can be applied in the most reliable manner, typically the less complex party in the controlled transaction. (3) Arm's length range: Defines the arm's length range as a range of financial results (e.g., prices, profit margins, rates of return) derived from two or more uncontrolled transactions that meet the comparability requirements for a given method. If the controlled transaction falls within the arm's length range, no Section 482 adjustment is warranted. If the result falls outside the range, the IRS may adjust the controlled taxpayer's results to the median of the range. (4) Profit level indicators: Defines profit level indicators as ratios that measure the relationship of operating profit to an appropriate base (e.g., sales, costs, assets), used to evaluate whether the results of a controlled transaction are consistent with the arm's length standard. Common profit level indicators include the ratio of operating profit to sales, gross profit to operating expenses (Berry ratio), and return on assets. These definitions align with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II and III concepts regarding selection of the tested party, comparable uncontrolled transactions, and financial indicators used in the transactional net margin method (TNMM) and other methods.",
