      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "Profit Level Indicators for Comparable Profits Method",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(i) identifies profit level indicators used to measure arm's length profitability in the context of the comparable profits method and related transactional profit methods. The specified profit level indicators include: (i) Rate of return on capital employed — measuring operating profit relative to the capital base deployed in the controlled transaction; (ii) Financial ratios — quantitative relationships between financial statement line items used to assess profitability and operational efficiency relative to comparables; and (iii) Other profit level indicators — a residual category permitting the use of additional measures of profitability that may be appropriate depending on the facts and circumstances of the controlled transaction and the availability of reliable comparable data. These indicators serve as benchmarking tools to evaluate whether the results of a controlled transaction are consistent with the arm's length standard by comparing the tested party's profitability against that of uncontrolled comparables performing similar functions, bearing similar risks, and employing similar assets. The selection of an appropriate profit level indicator requires consideration of the reliability of available data, the degree of functional comparability between the tested party and potential comparables, and the extent to which differences in accounting practices, business lines, or capital intensity may affect comparability. This provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on transactional profit methods, particularly the transactional net margin method (TNMM), which similarly employs net profit indicators such as return on assets, operating margins, and Berry ratios to assess arm's length outcomes.",
