      "id": "irc482-1c",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(c)",
      "title": "Comparability and Reliability Considerations — General Framework (§ 1.482-1(c))",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-1(c), the arm's length standard requires that the results of a controlled transaction be consistent with the results that would have been realized if uncontrolled taxpayers had engaged in the same transaction under the same circumstances. To determine whether a controlled transaction produces arm's length results, the Commissioner evaluates the comparability of the controlled transaction to uncontrolled transactions. A transaction is comparable to a controlled transaction if the results of applying the chosen transfer pricing method to the uncontrolled transaction would be reliable for purposes of establishing an arm's length result. The degree of comparability between controlled and uncontrolled transactions depends upon the relevant facts and circumstances. Factors relevant to comparability include: (i) the functions performed by the parties, (ii) the contractual terms of the transactions, (iii) the risks assumed by the parties, (iv) the economic conditions in which the transactions occur, and (v) the nature of the property transferred or services provided. The reliability of a method also depends on the completeness and accuracy of the data used, the reliability of assumptions made, and the sensitivity of the results to deficiencies in the data or assumptions. Where significant comparability differences exist between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions, adjustments must be made to account for those differences, or a different uncontrolled transaction or method must be selected. The two primary considerations — comparability and reliability — operate together to identify the best method for establishing an arm's length result under the best method rule of § 1.482-1(c)(1).",
