      "id": "irc482-1i",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "Comparability Analysis Framework: Functional, Risk, Resource, and Other Factors Under § 1.482-1(i)",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(i) establishes the comparability analysis framework for determining whether controlled and uncontrolled transactions yield arm's length results. The framework comprises four principal components: (i) general comparability principles requiring that controlled transactions be compared to uncontrolled transactions under comparable circumstances; (ii) functional, risk, and resource comparability, requiring analysis of the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed by the tested party and potential comparables — including manufacturing, distribution, R&D, marketing, and financing functions, as well as market, credit, product, and foreign currency risks; (iii) other comparability factors, encompassing contractual terms, economic conditions, and property or services at issue that may affect the reliability of a comparison; and (iv) adjustments for differences between the tested party and uncontrolled taxpayers, requiring that material differences affecting price or profit be identified and, where possible, quantified through reasonably accurate adjustments. The data and assumptions underlying the comparability analysis must be reliable, contemporaneous, and reflective of actual economic conditions at the time of the controlled transaction. The reliability of a transfer pricing method increases as the degree of comparability between controlled and uncontrolled transactions increases and as the quality of adjustments for differences improves. This framework aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I (particularly paragraphs 1.33–1.118) on comparability analysis, including the five comparability factors: characteristics of property or services, functional analysis, contractual terms, economic circumstances, and business strategies.",
