      "title": "Transfer Pricing Methods — Profit Split Method Reference under Arm's Length Standard",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(e) directs attention to § 1.482-6, which sets forth the profit split method as one of the applicable transfer pricing methods for determining arm's length results in controlled transactions. The profit split method evaluates whether the allocation of the combined operating profit or loss attributable to one or more controlled transactions is arm's length by reference to the relative value of each controlled taxpayer's contribution to that combined operating profit or loss. This method is particularly relevant where transactions are so interrelated that they cannot be evaluated separately, or where both parties to a transaction contribute valuable and unique intangibles. The profit split method may be applied using either a comparable profit split approach, which compares the division of profits to that achieved by uncontrolled taxpayers in comparable transactions, or a residual profit split approach, which first allocates routine returns to each party and then splits any residual profit based on relative contributions of valuable intangibles or other non-routine resources. The method aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the transactional profit split method, recognizing its utility in highly integrated operations and multi-sided contributions. Application requires identification of the relevant business activity generating combined profits, allocation of costs, assets, and revenues to that activity, and determination of each party's relative contribution through analysis of functions performed, risks assumed, and assets employed.",
