      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(b)",
      "title": "Arm's Length Standard — Appropriate Allocation of Profits and Losses Among Controlled Taxpayers",
      "text": "Section 1.482-1(b) sets forth the foundational arm's length standard requiring that the true taxable income of a controlled taxpayer be determined so that it reflects the allocation of income, deductions, credits, and allowances between or among controlled entities as if those entities were uncontrolled parties dealing at arm's length. The provision establishes that each controlled taxpayer must receive its appropriate share of profits and losses from controlled transactions. The standard requires that the terms and conditions of controlled transactions — including prices, compensation, and other financial arrangements — must be consistent with those that would have been agreed upon between unrelated parties in comparable transactions under comparable circumstances. This standard governs all categories of controlled transactions, including transfers of tangible property, intangible property, services, loans, and cost sharing arrangements. The arm's length standard is the benchmark against which the Commissioner may allocate gross income, deductions, credits, or allowances between or among controlled entities when such allocations are necessary to prevent the evasion of taxes or to clearly reflect the income of any of those entities. This provision aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Article 1 arm's length principle, codifying the requirement that intercompany pricing arrangements replicate outcomes achievable in the open market between independent enterprises. Controlled taxpayers bear the burden of demonstrating that their pricing satisfies this standard through contemporaneous documentation, selection of the best method, and application of comparability analysis.",
