      "title": "Profit Split Method — Comparability, Reliability, and Residual Profit Split Framework",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-1(i), the profit split method evaluates whether the allocation of combined operating profit or loss attributable to one or more controlled transactions is arm's length by reference to the relative value of each controlled taxpayer's contribution to that combined operating profit or loss. The method requires analysis of comparability and reliability considerations, including: (A) general standards for evaluating the method's applicability; (B) comparability factors, encompassing (1) general comparability principles and (2) required adjustments for material differences between controlled and uncontrolled taxpayers or transactions; (C) the quality and reliability of the data and assumptions used in the analysis; and (D) other factors affecting the overall reliability of the profit split result. The residual profit split variant under subsection (3) allocates combined profit in two stages — first, routine returns are allocated to each participant based on market returns for similar functions performed without use of valuable intangibles; second, any residual profit (attributable to non-routine contributions, typically valuable intangibles) is allocated among the controlled parties based on the relative value of their non-routine contributions. Comparability under the profit split method focuses on the functions performed, risks assumed, resources employed, and contractual terms of the controlled arrangement relative to uncontrolled arrangements. Adjustments must be made for any differences that could materially affect the profit split result. The reliability of the method depends on the completeness and accuracy of the data available and the reasonableness of the assumptions underlying the allocation of combined profits.",
