      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-1(i)",
      "title": "Profit Split Method — Two-Step Allocation of Combined Operating Profit",
      "text": "Under the profit split method, the combined operating profit (or loss) from controlled transactions is allocated between the controlled taxpayers in a two-step process. Step one allocates income to routine contributions: each party receives a market return for its routine, non-unique contributions of functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed, benchmarked to comparable uncontrolled transactions. Step two allocates residual profit: any remaining combined profit (or loss), after accounting for routine returns, is divided among the parties based on the relative value of their non-routine contributions — typically unique and valuable intangibles or other contributions not adequately reflected in the routine return. Comparability and reliability of the profit split depend on: (A) the overall quality of the analysis, including whether the method reflects the economic reality of the transaction; (B) the degree of comparability between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions with respect to functions, risks, contractual terms, economic conditions, and property or services involved; (C) the quality and reliability of the data and assumptions used to determine each party's routine and residual contributions; and (D) other factors affecting reliability, including the extent to which the analysis captures all material contributions of value. The profit split method is generally most reliable where both parties make unique and valuable contributions to the controlled transactions, or where the transactions are so highly integrated that separate pricing is impractical. The method aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, Part III (transactional profit split method).",
