      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(a)",
      "title": "Arm's Length Interest Rate on Intercompany Loans and Advances",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-2(a), where one member of a group of controlled entities makes a loan or advance to another member of the group, the IRS may make allocations to reflect an arm's length rate of interest on such loan or advance. The provision addresses bona fide indebtedness between controlled parties and establishes that the consideration charged for the use of money must meet the arm's length standard. Where no interest is charged, or where interest is charged at a rate that does not reflect an arm's length result, the district director may make appropriate allocations to reflect an arm's length rate. The provision distinguishes between situations where an applicable federal rate (AFR) safe harbor applies and situations requiring a more comprehensive arm's length analysis. Bona fide indebtedness must be established as a genuine debt obligation rather than a disguised equity contribution or other arrangement. The arm's length rate is the rate of interest that was charged, or would have been charged, at the time the indebtedness arose, in independent transactions with or between unrelated parties under similar circumstances. Relevant factors include the principal amount, maturity, collateral, credit standing of the borrower, and prevailing market conditions.",
