      "text": "Section 1.482-2(i) establishes arm's length standards and safe haven interest rate provisions for loans and advances between controlled entities. The general rule requires that interest charged on intercompany loans reflect an arm's length rate. A safe haven is provided for certain loans and advances made after May 8, 1986, under which interest rates within a range based on the Applicable Federal Rate (AFR) will be respected as arm's length. The safe haven applies as a general rule and includes a grandfather rule for existing loans predating the provision. The AFR, as published by the IRS, serves as the benchmark for determining the safe haven range. Special rules apply where the lender is in the business of making loans, and separate provisions address foreign currency-denominated loans. Additionally, the provision coordinates interest adjustments with those required under certain other Internal Revenue Code sections to avoid duplicative adjustments. Funds obtained at the situs of the borrower are also addressed, recognizing that the economic circumstances of the borrowing entity's location may affect the appropriate arm's length rate. The provision includes illustrative examples demonstrating application of the safe haven rates across various fact patterns.",
