      "text": "Under § 1.482-2(b), where one member of a group of controlled entities performs services for the benefit of another member, an arm's length charge must be made for such services. The provision establishes a benefit test requiring that services actually confer an economic or commercial benefit on the recipient before a charge is warranted. The arm's length charge is determined by reference to the amount that would have been charged by an uncontrolled party performing comparable services under comparable circumstances. Costs or deductions properly allocable to the performance of services are taken into account in determining the arm's length charge, while certain costs and deductions — such as those that do not directly relate to the services rendered — are excluded. The provision identifies permissible methods for determining the appropriate intercompany services charge, addresses certain categories of services that receive specific treatment, and governs services rendered in connection with the transfer of property, ensuring that the services component is properly separated from and priced consistently with the property transfer component. The overarching objective is to prevent undercharging or overcharging for intercompany services that would distort taxable income between controlled entities. This framework aligns broadly with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter VII on intercompany services, including the threshold inquiry of whether a service has been rendered and the benefit test applied to the recipient.",
