      "title": "Arm's Length Charge for Use of Tangible Property Between Controlled Entities",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(c) establishes the general rule and arm's length charge standard for the use of tangible property between controlled entities. Under the general rule (§ 1.482-2(c)(1)), where one member of a group of controlled entities allows another member to use tangible property without charge, or at a charge that is not consistent with an arm's length result, the IRS may make allocations to reflect an arm's length charge for such use. Under § 1.482-2(c)(2), the arm's length charge for the use of tangible property is the amount that would have been charged by an uncontrolled party for use of the same or similar property under the same or similar circumstances, taking into account all relevant facts and conditions. This includes consideration of the type and condition of the property, the duration of use, the functions performed and risks assumed by each party, and comparable uncontrolled transactions for the use or lease of similar property. The provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter VI principles regarding the importance of arm's length conditions in intercompany arrangements involving property, and is consistent with OECD guidance on comparable uncontrolled price methodology applied to rental or lease transactions. Failure to charge an arm's length amount for the use of tangible property between controlled parties may trigger adjustments under the broader Section 482 authority and expose the taxpayer to transfer pricing penalties under Section 6662(e) and (h).",
