      "id": "irc482-2b",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(b)",
      "title": "Comparable Uncontrolled Price Method for Loans and Advances — § 1.482-2(b)",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(b) provides the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method for determining arm's length interest rates on loans and advances between controlled parties. Under this method, the arm's length interest rate is determined by reference to the interest rate charged in comparable uncontrolled transactions. A comparable uncontrolled transaction is one in which the lender or borrower is involved in a loan transaction with an unrelated party, and the terms and conditions are sufficiently similar to permit a meaningful comparison. Comparability is assessed by reference to factors including the principal amount and duration of the loan, the credit standing of the borrower, the nature and degree of any security, the currency involved, and the risks assumed by the lender. Where differences exist between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions, adjustments must be made to account for the effect of those differences on the interest rate, provided such adjustments can be made with sufficient accuracy. The reliability of the CUP method increases as the degree of comparability between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions increases. If no comparable uncontrolled transactions exist, or if adjustments for differences cannot be made with sufficient reliability, the CUP method will not produce a reliable measure of the arm's length result, and alternative methods should be considered. The method aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines' comparable uncontrolled price method as applied to financial transactions, recognizing that the arm's length rate must reflect market conditions for loans with comparable characteristics.",
