      "text": "Section 1.482-2(i) establishes the general framework for applying the arm's length standard to controlled transactions. It addresses: (i) the general arm's length principle requiring that the results of controlled transactions reflect the terms and conditions that would have been agreed upon by uncontrolled parties under similar circumstances; (ii) comparability analysis, including (A) the general standard for determining whether controlled and uncontrolled transactions are comparable, and (B) the requirement to make adjustments for material differences between controlled and uncontrolled transactions that affect price or profit; (iii) the data and assumptions underlying comparability determinations, including the use of available data and the standard of reasonableness in selecting and applying that data. The provision also establishes (3) the arm's length range concept, under which a set of results derived from multiple comparable uncontrolled transactions may define an acceptable range of arm's length outcomes, and any controlled transaction result falling within this range is treated as arm's length; (4) examples illustrating the application of these principles; and (5) the permissible use of indirect evidence of comparable uncontrolled transactions where direct comparables are unavailable, subject to appropriate reliability considerations. These provisions collectively implement the arm's length standard consistent with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I principles on comparability and the arm's length principle.",
