      "title": "Resale Price Method for Tangible Property Transfers — § 1.482-2(c)",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-2(c), the resale price method evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction for tangible property is arm's length by reference to the gross profit margin earned by a comparable uncontrolled reseller. The arm's length price is determined by starting with the applicable resale price at which the property purchased in the controlled transaction is resold by the controlled buyer to an uncontrolled purchaser, and then reducing that price by an appropriate gross profit margin. The appropriate gross profit margin is derived from comparable uncontrolled transactions in which the reseller performs similar functions, bears similar risks, and employs similar assets. The resale price method is ordinarily the best method when the reseller adds relatively little value to the product and does not substantially alter the property prior to resale. Comparability under this method depends principally on similarity of functions performed, risks assumed, and contractual terms, and is less sensitive to product similarity than the comparable uncontrolled price method. Adjustments may be required to account for differences in functions, risks, contractual terms, or economic conditions between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions. This method aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II resale price method, which similarly focuses on the gross margin earned by the reseller as the primary comparability metric.",
