      "title": "Resale Price Method for Controlled Transfers of Tangible Property",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(i) establishes the resale price method (RPM) as an applicable transfer pricing methodology for determining the arm's length price in controlled transactions involving tangible property. The method proceeds as follows: (i) In general — the resale price method evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction is arm's length by reference to the gross profit margin realized by a reseller in comparable uncontrolled transactions; (ii) Applicable resale price — the applicable resale price is the price at which property purchased in a controlled transaction is resold by the buyer to an uncontrolled party, used as the starting point for the backward derivation of the arm's length transfer price; (iii) Appropriate gross profit — the arm's length transfer price equals the applicable resale price reduced by an appropriate gross profit, which is derived from the gross profit margin earned by the tested party or comparable uncontrolled distributors in comparable uncontrolled transactions, reflecting functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed; (iv) Arm's length range — if the results of multiple comparable transactions fall within a range of appropriate gross profit margins, the controlled transaction satisfies the arm's length standard if it falls within that range; (3) Comparability and reliability considerations — the degree of comparability between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions is assessed with particular emphasis on functions performed, contractual terms, risks assumed, and economic conditions, with product similarity being less critical than functional comparability under the RPM. This method aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the resale price method.",
