      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(d)",
      "title": "Cost Plus Method for Controlled Transactions — § 1.482-2(d)",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(d) addresses the cost plus method as a means of determining an arm's length price for controlled transactions. Under the general framework, the cost plus method evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction is arm's length by reference to the gross profit markup earned by comparable uncontrolled sellers performing similar functions under similar circumstances. The determination of the arm's length price begins with the costs incurred by the seller in a controlled transaction and applies an appropriate gross profit markup, derived from comparable uncontrolled transactions, to arrive at an arm's length result. The method is particularly suited to transactions involving the manufacture, assembly, or other production of tangible goods that are then transferred to a related buyer. Comparability under this method focuses on the functions performed, risks assumed, and contractual terms of the controlled transaction relative to uncontrolled comparables, with particular emphasis on the consistency of accounting practices used to determine costs across the controlled and uncontrolled transactions. The gross profit markup applied must reflect the markup that would have been earned in comparable uncontrolled transactions, accounting for differences in product type, contractual terms, economic circumstances, and business strategies. Where reliable uncontrolled comparables are available, the cost plus method may yield the most accurate measure of an arm's length result; otherwise, adjustments may be required to account for material differences that affect comparability.",
