      "title": "Resale Price Method — Gross Profit and Arm's Length Range for Tangible Property Transfers",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(i) addresses the resale price method (RPM) for determining arm's length prices in controlled transactions involving tangible property. Under the RPM, the appropriate transfer price is determined by reference to the gross profit margin realized by a comparable uncontrolled reseller. The 'appropriate gross profit' is derived from comparable uncontrolled transactions, reflecting the reseller's functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed. An arm's length range is established from the gross profit margins of comparable uncontrolled transactions, and the controlled transaction's result is evaluated against this range. Comparability and reliability considerations require analysis of functional similarity between controlled and uncontrolled resellers, contractual terms, economic conditions, and the nature of property transferred. The method is most reliable when the reseller adds minimal value to the product before resale and when close functional comparables are available. Adjustments may be required to account for differences affecting gross profit margins between controlled and uncontrolled transactions.",
