      "title": "Services Transfer Pricing: Comparability, Data, and Assumptions Framework",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(i) establishes the analytical framework for evaluating controlled services transactions. The provision addresses: (i) the general standard for determining arm's length charges for services between controlled parties; (ii) comparability requirements, including (A) functional comparability, which requires analysis of the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed by each party in the controlled and uncontrolled service transactions; (B) other comparability factors such as contractual terms, economic conditions, and the nature and scope of the services; (C) adjustments for differences between controlled and uncontrolled transactions to improve comparability; and (D) the purchasing agent standard, which addresses situations where one party acts merely as an agent or conduit in procuring services for another; (iii) data and assumptions governing the analysis, including (A) general data requirements for establishing comparability and arm's length results, and (B) consistency in accounting, requiring that accounting methods and classifications used to determine costs and revenues be applied consistently across controlled and uncontrolled transactions to ensure meaningful comparisons. The provision concludes with illustrative examples demonstrating application of these standards. This framework aligns with OECD Guidelines Chapter VII principles on intra-group services, particularly the requirements for functional analysis and comparability in evaluating service charges.",
