      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(e)",
      "title": "Unspecified Methods for Intercompany Transfers — General Rule and Example",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(e) provides that where no specific method is prescribed under the section 482 regulations for a particular type of intercompany transaction, the IRS may still make allocations necessary to clearly reflect income. The provision establishes that taxpayers must nonetheless determine an arm's length result for transactions not covered by enumerated methods, applying the best method rule and using the most reliable means available to establish that controlled transactions reflect arm's length terms. The general rule in paragraph (1) confirms that the absence of a specified method does not exempt a transaction from section 482 scrutiny; rather, any reasonable method may be applied provided it produces results consistent with an arm's length standard. Paragraph (2) provides an illustrative example demonstrating how an unspecified method may be applied in practice, reinforcing that the arm's length standard governs regardless of whether a named method exists for the transaction type. This aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, which similarly permits other transactional profit methods when the five primary methods cannot be reliably applied.",
