      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(b)",
      "title": "Definition of Intangible Property for Transfer Pricing Purposes — §1.482-2(b)",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(b) provides the definitional framework for what constitutes 'intangible property' within the context of intercompany transfer pricing. Under this provision, intangible property encompasses a broad range of assets including patents, inventions, formulae, processes, designs, patterns, know-how, copyrights, literary, musical, or artistic compositions, trademarks, trade names, brand names, franchises, methods, programs, systems, procedures, campaigns, surveys, studies, forecasts, customer lists, technical data, and other similar items. The definition is significant because it establishes the threshold determination for whether a transfer or license of property between controlled parties is subject to the arm's length standard under §1.482-4 governing transfers of intangibles. An item qualifies as an intangible only if it has substantial value independent of the services of any individual. This definition is foundational to the application of the commensurate-with-income standard under §482, which requires that the arm's length consideration for a transfer of intangible property be commensurate with the income attributable to the intangible. The provision aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter VI (Special Considerations for Intangibles), which similarly requires identification of intangibles subject to arm's length analysis, including both trade intangibles and marketing intangibles. The definition also underpins cost sharing arrangements under §1.482-7, where the development of intangibles among controlled participants must be properly allocated.",
