      "title": "Comparable Uncontrolled Transaction (CUT) Method for Intangible Property Transfers — § 1.482-2(c)",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(c) establishes the Comparable Uncontrolled Transaction (CUT) method as a means of evaluating whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction involving the transfer of intangible property reflects an arm's length result. Under this method, the arm's length royalty rate or other consideration is determined by reference to the amount charged in a comparable uncontrolled transaction — i.e., a transaction between uncontrolled parties involving the transfer or license of the same or comparable intangible property under comparable circumstances. The provision requires analysis of comparability, considering factors such as the nature of the intangible, the terms of the transfer or license, the economic conditions, and the functions performed and risks assumed by the parties. Reliability of the CUT method depends on the degree of similarity between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions; adjustments may be made for material differences, but where such differences are significant and cannot be reliably adjusted for, the method's reliability is diminished. This method corresponds to the Comparable Uncontrolled Price (CUP) method applied to intangibles under the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter VI, and represents the most direct measure of an arm's length result when sufficiently comparable uncontrolled transactions are available.",
