      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(i)",
      "title": "Comparability Standards for Intercompany Loans and Advances — General Framework",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(i) establishes the general framework governing the determination of arm's length interest rates for loans and advances between controlled parties. The provision sets out the reliability hierarchy and comparability standards applicable to intercompany financing arrangements. Reliability criteria require that the method or rate selected most reliably reflects arm's length results given the facts and circumstances. Comparability analysis requires consideration of: (A) comparable intangible property (i.e., the terms and conditions of the debt instrument itself, including principal amount, maturity, security, and currency), and (B) comparable circumstances, encompassing the creditworthiness of the borrower, the lender's cost of funds, prevailing market conditions, and the economic functions performed by each party. Data and assumptions underlying any arm's length rate determination must be examined for their accuracy and reasonableness. The provision further requires determination of an arm's length range, within which the controlled interest rate must fall to avoid reallocation. The arm's length range is established using the most reliable data points from uncontrolled transactions or external market benchmarks. Illustrative examples are provided to demonstrate application of these standards. This framework aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter X (Financial Transactions), which similarly requires that the terms and conditions of intercompany financial transactions be benchmarked against economically comparable uncontrolled transactions, with particular attention to credit risk, functional profile of lender and borrower, and market conditions at the time of the transaction.",
