      "text": "Section 1.482-2(f) sets forth special rules applicable to transfers of intangible property between controlled entities. Subsection (f)(1) addresses the permissible forms of consideration in controlled transactions involving intangible property, requiring that the compensation reflect an arm's length charge consistent with the value of the intangible transferred or licensed. Subsection (f)(2) establishes the periodic adjustment mechanism, under which the IRS retains authority to adjust the consideration paid or received for an intangible transfer in subsequent years if the actual profits or benefits realized from the intangible deviate materially from those projected at the time of the transfer. This periodic adjustment rule is designed to ensure that the controlled taxpayer's return from an intangible transfer continues to reflect an arm's length result over the life of the arrangement, preventing artificial shifting of income attributable to high-value intangibles. The provision anticipates that intangible value is inherently uncertain at the time of transfer and that arm's length parties would typically include price adjustment mechanisms, such as contingent payment structures, to account for evolving profitability. Taxpayers relying on fixed-price arrangements must be prepared to demonstrate that the original pricing reflected realistic projections of the intangible's value and that the arm's length standard is satisfied throughout the term of the arrangement.",
