      "text": "Section 1.482-2(i) establishes the general rule for periodic adjustments to controlled transactions involving intangible property, along with enumerated exceptions and ownership provisions. Under the general rule, the IRS may make periodic adjustments to ensure that the consideration charged in a controlled transfer of intangible property reflects the actual profit experience attributable to the intangible over time. Exceptions to periodic adjustments include: (A) transactions involving the same intangible, where arm's length pricing has been established; (B) transactions involving a comparable intangible, where a comparable uncontrolled transaction provides a reliable benchmark; (C) use of methods other than the comparable uncontrolled transaction method, where an alternative method reliably determines arm's length results; (D) extraordinary events beyond the control of the parties that cause a material change in profitability; and (E) a five-year period limitation, after which periodic adjustments may no longer apply under specified conditions. The provision also addresses ownership of intangible property, governing which controlled party is treated as the legal and economic owner for purposes of determining the appropriate allocation of income attributable to the intangible. These rules are designed to prevent the underpricing of high-value intangibles in controlled transfers where actual commercialization results diverge significantly from projections at the time of the transaction, consistent with the commensurate-with-income standard under IRC § 482.",
