      "title": "Ownership and Compensation for Intangible Property Under Related-Party Arrangements",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(i) addresses the allocation of income and deductions with respect to intangible property transferred or used between controlled parties. It establishes rules for identifying the owner of intangible property, distinguishing between legally protected intangibles (e.g., patents, trademarks, copyrights) and intangibles that are not legally protected (e.g., know-how, trade secrets without formal registration). Where assistance is provided to the owner of intangible property by a related party, allocations may be required to reflect an arm's length charge for such assistance. The provision further specifies that consideration for the use of intangible property must not be artificially limited — meaning that the amount charged must reflect the full arm's length value of the intangible, regardless of contractual caps or artificial restrictions imposed between related parties. Lump sum payments for intangible property are also addressed, requiring that such payments reflect the present value of the reasonably anticipated benefits attributable to the intangible over its useful life. Examples are provided to illustrate the identification of owners and the appropriate allocation methodology when related-party assistance enhances or maintains the value of intangible property.",
