      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(i)",
      "title": "Comparable Profits Method — General Framework (§ 1.482-5)",
      "text": "Section 1.482-5 establishes the Comparable Profits Method (CPM) as an applicable transfer pricing method for determining arm's length results in controlled transactions. Under the CPM, the arm's length result is determined by reference to the amount of operating profit that the tested party would have earned on related party transactions if its profit level indicator (PLI) equaled that of an uncontrolled comparable. The method applies profit level indicators — such as return on assets, return on sales, or Berry ratio — derived from comparable uncontrolled taxpayers engaged in similar business activities under similar circumstances. The tested party is typically the party to the controlled transaction that is the least complex, has the fewest valuable intangibles, and for which reliable data can be identified. The comparable profits method is broadly analogous to the OECD's Transactional Net Margin Method (TNMM) under OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II. General exceptions and specific examples illustrate when the CPM may be applied or displaced by other methods, consistent with the best method rule under § 1.482-1(c). The regulation provides that the CPM may be used for transfers of tangible property, intangibles, and services where sufficient comparable uncontrolled data exist to compute reliable PLI ranges. Arm's length results must fall within the interquartile range of comparables or be adjusted to the median of that range.",
