      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(b)",
      "title": "Arm's Length Result Determination and Tested Party Selection — Services and Loans (§ 1.482-2(b))",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(b) establishes the framework for determining an arm's length result in the context of transactions between controlled taxpayers, with particular attention to the identification of the tested party. Under paragraph (b)(1), the arm's length result is determined by reference to the results of comparable transactions between uncontrolled taxpayers under comparable circumstances. The regulation requires that the method selected produce a result consistent with what would have been realized had the controlled taxpayer dealt with an uncontrolled taxpayer at arm's length. Under paragraph (b)(2), the tested party is generally the participant in the controlled transaction whose operating profit attributable to the controlled transaction can be verified using the most reliable data and requiring the fewest and most reliable adjustments. The tested party will often be the least complex of the controlled taxpayers, lacking valuable intangible property or unique assets that distinguish it from potential uncontrolled comparables. The selection of the tested party is a threshold determination that governs the subsequent application of profit-based methods, particularly the comparable profits method (CPM), and directly influences comparability analysis and the reliability of the transfer pricing result. This provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter III guidance on the selection of the tested party and the principle that the method and tested party together should yield the most reliable measure of an arm's length outcome.",
