      "id": "irc482-2i",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(i)",
      "title": "Comparable Profits Method — General Framework, Tested Party, Arm's Length Range, and Profit Level Indicators",
      "text": "Section 1.482-5 sets forth the comparable profits method (CPM) as an applicable method for evaluating whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction is arm's length. The CPM evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction is arm's length based on objective measures of profitability (profit level indicators) derived from uncontrolled taxpayers that engage in similar business activities under similar circumstances. (i) In general: The CPM measures the combined operating profit of the tested party against that earned by comparable uncontrolled taxpayers. (ii) Adjustments for tested party: The tested party is the participant in the controlled transaction whose operating profit attributable to the controlled transactions can be verified using the most reliable data and requiring the fewest and most reliable adjustments. The tested party is typically the less complex of the controlled taxpayers and does not own valuable intangibles or unique assets that distinguish it from potential comparables. (3) Arm's length range: An arm's length range is established using the profit level indicators of uncontrolled comparables. If the tested party's results fall within the arm's length range, no adjustment is warranted. If outside the range, the Commissioner may adjust the tested party's operating profit to the median of the range. (4) Profit level indicators: Profit level indicators are ratios that measure relationships between profits and costs incurred or resources employed. Appropriate profit level indicators include the ratio of operating profit to sales, the ratio of gross profit to operating expenses (Berry ratio), and the return on capital employed. The reliability of a profit level indicator depends on the extent to which it reflects the functions performed, assets employed, and risks assumed by the tested party.",
