      "text": "Under § 1.482-2(i), the following profit level indicators (PLIs) are enumerated for use in evaluating the arm's length character of controlled service transactions: (i) Rate of return on capital employed — measures the return earned on assets deployed in the provision of services, reflecting capital intensity and asset utilization; (ii) Financial ratios — includes metrics such as gross margin, operating margin, and other income-statement-based ratios that benchmark profitability relative to revenues or costs; (iii) Other profit level indicators — a residual category permitting the use of additional PLIs appropriate to the facts and circumstances of the controlled transaction, consistent with the selection criteria applicable to comparable uncontrolled transactions. These PLIs are used in conjunction with transactional profit methods (analogous to the Transactional Net Margin Method under OECD Guidelines Chapter II, Part III) to test whether the compensation in a controlled services transaction reflects what uncontrolled parties would have agreed upon. The choice among PLIs must be supported by a comparability analysis that accounts for functional differences, asset intensity, and risk allocation between the tested party and comparable uncontrolled service providers.",
