      "title": "Comparability and Reliability Considerations for Intercompany Loans and Advances",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(c) establishes the comparability and reliability considerations applicable to controlled transactions involving loans or advances between related parties. Under the general rule, the arm's length interest rate for a controlled loan must be determined by reference to the rate that would have been charged in an uncontrolled transaction under the same or similar circumstances. Comparability analysis requires evaluation of factors including the principal amount and duration of the loan, the creditworthiness of the borrower, the nature and purpose of the loan, the collateral provided, the currency of the loan, and the economic conditions prevailing at the time the loan was made. Reliability of a comparable uncontrolled transaction depends on the degree of similarity between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions, and the extent to which reliable adjustments can be made to account for material differences. Where a comparable uncontrolled transaction cannot be identified, the arm's length rate may be determined by reference to market data, such as publicly available interest rate indices, provided that appropriate adjustments are made to reflect the specific characteristics of the controlled transaction. The provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter X (Financial Transactions, 2020) regarding the determination of arm's length conditions for intragroup financial transactions, including the importance of the borrower's credit rating, loan terms, and prevailing market conditions.",
