      "text": "Section 1.482-2(i) establishes the comparability analysis framework applicable to controlled transactions. The provision addresses: (i) general comparability principles requiring that results of controlled transactions reflect arm's length outcomes achievable between uncontrolled parties under comparable circumstances; (ii) functional, risk, and resource comparability, requiring analysis of the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed by each party to the controlled transaction as compared to uncontrolled taxpayers; (iii) other comparability factors, including contractual terms, economic conditions, and the nature of the property or services involved; and (iv) adjustments for differences between the tested party and uncontrolled taxpayers, providing that if material differences exist that would affect the measure of arm's length results, adjustments must be made to account for those differences, provided that reliable adjustments can be made. The provision also addresses the data and assumptions used in applying the comparability analysis, requiring that the most reliable data available be used and that assumptions be consistent with the facts and circumstances of the controlled transaction. This framework aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I comparability factors, including functional analysis, contractual terms, risk allocation, economic circumstances, and business strategies.",
