      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(e)",
      "title": "Profit Split Method — Cross-Reference and Introductory Provision",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(e) provides illustrative examples relevant to the application of transfer pricing methods, and cross-references § 1.482-6, which governs the profit split method. The profit split method evaluates whether the allocation of the combined operating profit or loss attributable to one or more controlled transactions is arm's length by reference to the relative value of each controlled taxpayer's contribution to that combined operating profit or loss. Under § 1.482-6, the profit split method may be applied using either a comparable profit split approach, which derives the allocation from the way uncontrolled taxpayers split profits in comparable transactions, or a residual profit split approach, which first allocates income to routine contributions and then splits any residual profit based on the relative value of non-routine contributions, typically intangibles. The method is generally applied where transactions are so interrelated that they cannot be evaluated separately, or where both parties to a transaction own valuable non-routine intangible property. The profit split method aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, Part III (profit split method), reflecting the internationally recognized approach to allocating combined profits among associated enterprises based on economically valid criteria.",
