      "title": "Appropriate Share of Profits and Losses Under Controlled Transactions",
      "text": "§ 1.482-2(b) provides that in any transaction between controlled taxpayers, the true taxable income of each controlled taxpayer must reflect an appropriate share of the profits and losses from the transaction. Where the consideration paid in a controlled transaction does not reflect an arm's length result, the Commissioner may make allocations to reflect the appropriate share of profits and losses that would have been realized in an arm's length transaction between uncontrolled parties. This provision establishes the foundational principle that controlled parties must not shift profits inappropriately among related entities, and that each party to a controlled transaction must bear its appropriate share of the economic risks and rewards commensurate with its functions, assets, and risks. The allocation of profits and losses must reflect the economic substance of the arrangement, taking into account the functions performed, risks assumed, and assets employed by each controlled taxpayer. Where the allocation of profits and losses between controlled parties does not reflect arm's length dealing, adjustments may be made to ensure that each party's taxable income reflects the consideration that would have been paid or received by an uncontrolled taxpayer in comparable circumstances. This principle aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I, which establishes that associated enterprises should be treated as separate entities dealing at arm's length, with profits allocated in accordance with the actual functional and risk profiles of each entity.",
