      "title": "Allocation of Income and Deductions — Loans and Services: Application of Comparable Profit Split",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(c) governs the application of transfer pricing rules to specific intercompany transactions, including loans and services. Subsection (c)(1) establishes the general framework for applying arm's length standards to such transactions, requiring that the amount charged in any intercompany transaction be consistent with what uncontrolled taxpayers would charge under comparable circumstances. Subsection (c)(2) introduces the comparable profit split method as an applicable methodology, under which income from controlled transactions is allocated between related parties based on the relative contributions of each party, benchmarked against the profit splits observed in comparable uncontrolled transactions. This method is applied where two or more controlled taxpayers each make significant contributions to the controlled transaction, and where the combined operating profit or loss can be identified and reliably measured. The comparable profit split requires identification of an appropriate profit level indicator, determination of the combined operating profit attributable to the controlled transactions, and allocation of that profit in proportion to the parties' relative contributions as evidenced by comparable uncontrolled profit splits. This approach aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the profit split method, particularly where both parties contribute unique and valuable intangibles or assume economically significant risks such that a one-sided method would be unreliable.",
