      "title": "Profit Split Method — Comparability, Reliability, and Residual Profit Split Framework",
      "text": "Section 1.482-2(i) establishes the framework for applying the profit split method to controlled transactions. Under the general provisions, the method allocates the combined operating profit or loss from controlled transactions among the participants in a manner that reflects the relative value of each party's contribution. The comparability and reliability considerations require evaluation of whether the profit split produces an arm's length result. In general, comparability under this method focuses on the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed by each controlled taxpayer. Adjustments must be made for differences between controlled and uncontrolled taxpayers that materially affect the reliability of the method. The data and assumptions used must be sufficiently complete and accurate to support the allocation of combined profits. Other factors affecting reliability include the quality of available data, the consistency of accounting practices across participants, and the extent to which the method reliably measures each party's relative contribution to value creation. The residual profit split provision addresses situations where the initial allocation accounts for routine contributions of each participant using market-based returns, with any residual profit or loss — attributable to non-routine contributions such as valuable intangibles — allocated among the controlled taxpayers based on the relative value of their non-routine contributions. This two-step approach aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the transactional profit split method, which similarly separates routine from non-routine returns and allocates residual profits based on the relative value of unique and valuable intangible contributions.",
