      "id": "irc482-2i",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-2(i)",
      "title": "Profit Split Method — Residual Profit Allocation Under §1.482-2(i)",
      "text": "Under §1.482-2(i), the profit split method allocates combined operating profit (or loss) from controlled transactions in two steps. First, routine contributions by each party are allocated a market return benchmarked against comparable uncontrolled transactions (the 'routine return' allocation). Second, any residual profit remaining after routine returns are assigned is allocated among the controlled parties based on the relative value of their non-routine contributions, typically intangible property or unique functions that cannot be reliably benchmarked. Comparability and reliability considerations require that: (A) the method be applied using the most reliable data available; (B) comparability be assessed with respect to functions performed, risks assumed, and contractual terms, consistent with the functional analysis; (C) data and assumptions used to measure each party's contributions be reasonable and supportable; and (D) other factors affecting reliability, including the consistency of accounting practices across entities and the availability of external benchmarks, be evaluated. The profit split method is most reliable where both parties make significant non-routine contributions (e.g., valuable intangibles), making one-sided methods inappropriate. This approach aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, Part III (Transactional Profit Split Method), which similarly prescribes a two-step residual analysis when unique and valuable contributions preclude the use of traditional transaction methods.",
