      "title": "Transfer Pricing Methods for Transfers of Tangible Property — General Rule",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(a) establishes the general rule governing the determination of arm's length prices for controlled transfers of tangible property. The Commissioner will determine the true taxable income of a controlled taxpayer from a transfer of tangible property by applying the method that, under the facts and circumstances, provides the most reliable measure of an arm's length result. The methods available for determining arm's length prices for transfers of tangible property are: (1) the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method, described in paragraph (b); (2) the resale price method (RPM), described in paragraph (c); (3) the cost plus method (CPM), described in paragraph (d); (4) the comparable profits method, described in § 1.482-5; (5) the profit split method, described in § 1.482-6; and (6) unspecified methods, described in paragraph (e). The best method rule of § 1.482-1(c) must be applied to select the method that provides the most reliable measure of an arm's length result, taking into account the degree of comparability between the controlled transaction and uncontrolled comparables, the quality of data available, and the reliability of assumptions required.",
