      "id": "irc482-3b",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(b)",
      "title": "Comparable Uncontrolled Price (CUP) Method for Tangible Property Transfers",
      "text": "§ 1.482-3(b) establishes the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method as a means of evaluating whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction involving the transfer of tangible property is arm's length. Under this method, the arm's length price is determined by reference to the price charged in a comparable uncontrolled transaction — an 'internal CUP' where the tested party itself is a party to the uncontrolled transaction, or an 'external CUP' where the price derives from third-party market transactions. The CUP method produces the most reliable measure of an arm's length result when (i) there is an uncontrolled transaction that is identical to the controlled transaction, or (ii) any differences between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions either have no material effect on price or can be reflected through reasonably accurate adjustments. Comparability under § 1.482-3(b)(2) requires analysis of product characteristics (physical features, quality, reliability, availability, and volume), contractual terms (including warranties, credit and payment terms, and transportation terms), economic conditions (including market level, geographic market, and timing), and the functions performed and risks assumed by each party. Where differences exist between controlled and uncontrolled transactions, the taxpayer must make adjustments to account for those differences when the effect on price can be determined with reasonable accuracy. The reliability of the CUP is enhanced when product similarity is high and differences are minor. Consistent with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, the CUP is the preferred method when sufficiently comparable uncontrolled prices exist, as it provides a direct market-based reference that minimises reliance on profit-based approximations.",
