      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(i)",
      "title": "Resale Price Method — Comparability, Data, and Arm's Length Range Requirements for Tangible Property Transfers",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-3(i), the resale price method (RPM) evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction for tangible property is arm's length by reference to the gross profit margin earned by the reseller in comparable uncontrolled transactions. Comparability under the RPM is assessed by examining functional similarity between the controlled and uncontrolled reseller, particularly reselling activities, with less emphasis on product similarity than under the comparable uncontrolled price method. Adjustments must be made for differences between controlled and uncontrolled transactions that materially affect the gross profit margin, including differences in contractual terms, economic conditions, levels of the market, and inventory risks. Data and assumptions underlying the RPM analysis must be reliable, and where possible, multiple-year data should be used to account for business cycles. The arm's length range is established from the results of multiple comparable uncontrolled transactions that satisfy the comparability standards, and a taxpayer's result falling outside the range may be subject to adjustment. Indirect evidence of comparable uncontrolled transactions may be used when direct comparables are unavailable, subject to appropriate reliability weighting. The method is most reliable when the reseller adds little value beyond distribution functions and when uncontrolled comparables perform substantially similar functions without material differences requiring adjustments.",
