      "text": "Section 1.482-3(i) addresses the use of unspecified methods for determining arm's length prices in controlled transactions involving transfers of tangible property. Paragraph (i)(i) sets out the general rule permitting the use of methods not explicitly enumerated in § 1.482-3 (i.e., methods other than CUP, resale price, cost plus, comparable profits, or profit split) where such methods produce the most reliable measure of an arm's length result under the best method rule of § 1.482-1(c). Paragraph (i)(ii) establishes limitations on the application of unspecified methods, requiring that any unspecified method must still satisfy the arm's length standard and that its reliability is assessed relative to specified methods using the same comparability and data-quality standards. Paragraph (i)(iii) provides illustrative examples demonstrating how the general rule and limitations apply in practice to controlled transfers of tangible property. Together, these provisions ensure flexibility in transfer pricing analysis while preserving the primacy of arm's length principles and the best method rule, consistent with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the hierarchy and selection of transfer pricing methods.",
