      "text": "§ 1.482-3(c) provides the resale price method (RPM) as an applicable method for determining the arm's length price in controlled transactions involving transfers of tangible property. Under the RPM, the arm's length price paid by a tested party acting as a reseller is determined by starting with the applicable resale price — generally the price at which the reseller sells the purchased property to an uncontrolled party — and reducing that price by an appropriate gross profit margin (the 'resale price margin'). The resale price margin reflects the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed by the reseller, benchmarked against the gross profit margins earned by comparable uncontrolled resellers in comparable transactions. The resulting figure, after accounting for other relevant costs and adjustments, represents the arm's length price for the controlled transfer. The method is particularly suitable where the tested party performs distribution or resale functions without adding significant value to the property through further processing or the contribution of intangibles. Adjustments may be required to account for differences in accounting practices, contractual terms, functions performed, risks assumed, and market conditions between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions to achieve reliable comparability. The RPM aligns with Chapter II of the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines, which similarly recognizes the resale price method as a one-sided transactional method appropriate for distribution arrangements. Where the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method is not applicable, the RPM may provide the most reliable measure of an arm's length result for routine resale activities.",
