      "title": "Resale Price Method — General Framework and Comparability Requirements",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(i) establishes the resale price method (RPM) as an applicable method for determining the arm's length price for transfers of tangible property between controlled parties. Under the RPM, the arm's length price is determined by reference to the applicable resale price — the price at which the buyer resells the property to an uncontrolled party — reduced by an appropriate gross profit margin. The appropriate gross profit margin represents the amount of gross profit that would allow the reseller to recover its operating expenses and earn an arm's length return on its functions performed, assets employed, and risks assumed. The arm's length range is established by reference to the gross profit margins earned by uncontrolled distributors performing comparable functions under comparable circumstances. Comparability and reliability considerations require analysis of whether the controlled and uncontrolled transactions involve similar functions, contractual terms, risks, economic conditions, and property or services. The RPM is most reliable when the reseller adds relatively little value to the product and the time elapsed between purchase and resale is short, limiting the impact of market fluctuations. Functional differences that materially affect gross profit margins must be accounted for through adjustments. Inventory, warranty, and currency risk assumption are among the key factors affecting comparability. The reliability of the RPM is enhanced when accounting practices and product similarity between tested and comparable transactions are closely aligned.",
