      "text": "Under § 1.482-3(b), the resale price method (RPM) evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transfer of tangible property is arm's length by reference to the gross profit margin realized by an uncontrolled reseller in a comparable uncontrolled transaction. Comparability under the RPM focuses primarily on functional comparability: the reseller's functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed must be comparable to those of the controlled reseller, as differences in these factors most directly affect the gross profit margin earned. Other comparability factors—including product similarity, contractual terms, economic conditions, and business strategies—must also be assessed, though product similarity is generally less critical under the RPM than under the comparable uncontrolled price method. Where differences exist between controlled and uncontrolled transactions that materially affect gross profit margins, adjustments must be made to improve comparability. A controlled taxpayer functioning as a sales agent may require special analysis to determine whether the RPM or another method is more appropriate. With respect to data and assumptions, the RPM requires consistent accounting practices between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions so that the gross profit margins being compared reflect equivalent treatment of costs. Where accounting differences exist, appropriate adjustments must be made. The regulation is illustrated by examples demonstrating the application of these comparability and data-consistency principles. This methodology aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the resale price method, which similarly emphasizes functional comparability over product comparability and requires accounting consistency when applying gross margin benchmarks.",
