      "text": "The cost plus method evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction for tangible property is arm's length by reference to the gross profit markup realized by the seller on uncontrolled transactions. The arm's length price is determined by adding to the seller's cost of producing the transferred property a markup sufficient to allow the seller to earn a profit reflecting the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed in the controlled transaction, compared to the markups earned in comparable uncontrolled transactions. The cost plus method is ordinarily appropriate where a manufacturer or producer sells tangible goods to a related purchaser, particularly in contract manufacturing, assembly, or processing arrangements. The comparability analysis focuses primarily on functional similarity between the tested party's controlled activities and those of comparable uncontrolled manufacturers or processors, including similarity in cost structures, manufacturing complexity, and value-added content. Differences in accounting practices that materially affect the cost base must be identified and adjusted before computing the markup. Where a comparable uncontrolled price cannot be established, the cost plus markup is benchmarked against markups earned by comparable uncontrolled manufacturers performing similar functions. The arm's length range is established by reference to the interquartile range of markups from the set of comparable uncontrolled transactions or comparable uncontrolled parties. Results outside the arm's length range may be subject to adjustment by the IRS. This method aligns with the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the cost plus method as one of the traditional transaction methods.",
