      "title": "Resale Price Method — Comparability and Application Standards for Tangible Property Transfers",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-3(i), the resale price method (RPM) evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled purchase-resale transaction is arm's length by reference to the gross profit margin earned by the reseller in comparable uncontrolled transactions. Functional comparability is the primary comparability standard: because the reseller's gross profit margin must compensate for the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed, differences in these factors between controlled and uncontrolled transactions require careful analysis and may necessitate adjustments. Other comparability factors—including contractual terms, economic conditions, and property or services involved—are also assessed, though they generally carry less weight than functional comparability under this method. Where the reseller acts merely as a purchasing agent rather than bearing the risks and functions of an independent reseller, the method's application is adjusted accordingly. Data and assumptions underlying the RPM application must be consistent; in particular, accounting consistency is required so that gross profit margins are computed on the same basis across controlled and comparable uncontrolled transactions. Adjustments must be made for material differences between controlled and uncontrolled transactions that would affect gross profit margins. The provision is illustrated by examples demonstrating application of these comparability and consistency principles to specific fact patterns involving tangible property transfers between related parties.",
