      "text": "Section 1.482-3(e) provides that methods not specifically enumerated in § 1.482-3 (i.e., methods other than the comparable uncontrolled price method, resale price method, cost plus method, comparable profits method, and profit split method) may be used to evaluate whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction involving tangible property reflects an arm's length result, provided such methods are applied in accordance with the best method rule of § 1.482-1(c). An unspecified method may be used only if it produces a result that is the most reliable measure of an arm's length result under the particular facts and circumstances. The method must be consistent with the arm's length standard and must account for all economically relevant characteristics of the transaction. Taxpayers relying on an unspecified method must demonstrate that no specified method produces a reliable measure of an arm's length result, and that the unspecified method provides a more reliable result than any specified method. The example provided illustrates circumstances in which an unspecified method may produce a result more reliable than any specified method, typically where the transaction involves unique characteristics that specified methods cannot adequately address. This provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on other transactional methods, which also recognize that no single method is suitable in all circumstances and that flexibility in method selection is appropriate where standard methods do not yield reliable results.",
