      "title": "Determination of Arm's Length Price for Transfers of Tangible Property — General Rule",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(a) provides the general rule for determining an arm's length price in controlled transactions involving the transfer of tangible property. The arm's length price must be determined under one of the following methods: (1) the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method, described in § 1.482-3(b); (2) the resale price method (RPM), described in § 1.482-3(c); (3) the cost plus method (CPM), described in § 1.482-3(d); (4) the comparable profits method (CPM), described in § 1.482-5; (5) the profit split method, described in § 1.482-6; or (6) an unspecified method, described in § 1.482-3(e). The best method rule of § 1.482-1(c) must be applied to select the method that, under the facts and circumstances, provides the most reliable measure of an arm's length result. No method is given priority over another, except that the comparable uncontrolled price method will generally be the most direct and reliable measure of an arm's length price for the transfer of tangible property when comparable uncontrolled transactions exist. The taxpayer must evaluate all available methods and apply the one that produces the most reliable result given the comparability of available data and the completeness and accuracy of that data.",
