      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(b)",
      "title": "Definition of Intangible Property for Transfer Pricing Purposes under § 1.482-3(b)",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(b) provides the definition of 'intangible' for purposes of applying the transfer pricing rules under IRC § 482. An intangible is an asset that comprises any of the following items and has substantial value independent of the services of any individual: patents, inventions, formulae, processes, designs, patterns, or know-how; copyrights and literary, musical, or artistic compositions; trademarks, trade names, or brand names; franchises, licenses, or contracts; methods, programs, systems, procedures, campaigns, surveys, studies, forecasts, estimates, customer lists, or technical data; and other similar items. The definition is relevant in determining whether a controlled transaction involves the transfer or use of an intangible, which triggers the application of the methods prescribed under § 1.482-4 for determining an arm's length price. The requirement that the asset have 'substantial value independent of the services of any individual' distinguishes compensable intangible property from mere human capital or workforce-in-place that may not independently give rise to a separate transfer pricing obligation. This definitional provision is foundational to identifying the subject matter of controlled transactions involving intangibles and underpins the selection of appropriate transfer pricing methods, including the comparable uncontrolled transaction (CUT) method, the comparable profits method (CPM), and the profit split method, as well as the application of the best method rule under § 1.482-1(c).",
