      "id": "irc482-3c",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(c)",
      "title": "Comparable Uncontrolled Transaction (CUT) Method for Transfers of Tangible Property",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(c) provides the comparable uncontrolled transaction (CUT) method as an approved method for determining the arm's length price in controlled transactions involving transfers of tangible property. Under the CUT method, the arm's length price is determined by reference to the amount charged in a comparable uncontrolled transaction — that is, a transaction between the controlled taxpayer and an uncontrolled party, or between two uncontrolled parties, that is comparable to the controlled transaction under review. A transaction is comparable if the property transferred and the circumstances of the transaction are sufficiently similar to those of the controlled transaction such that they provide a reliable measure of an arm's length result. Comparability is assessed under the general comparability standards of § 1.482-1(d), including the functions performed, risks assumed, contractual terms, economic conditions, and the nature of the property or services involved. Where there are material differences between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions, adjustments must be made to the extent that such differences would have a material effect on the price charged and the adjustments can be made with sufficient accuracy. The reliability of the CUT method depends on the degree of comparability between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions and the completeness and accuracy of data used. If close comparables are available, the CUT method will generally produce the most reliable measure of an arm's length result. If only inexact comparables exist, other methods or adjustments may improve reliability. This method aligns with the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method under OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, Section A.",
