      "id": "irc482-3i",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(i)",
      "title": "Profit Split Method — Framework and Comparability Requirements for Tangible Property Transfers",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(i) establishes the profit split method as an applicable method for determining arm's length prices in controlled transactions involving transfers of tangible property. Under this method, the combined operating profit or loss from controlled transactions is divided between the controlled taxpayers in a manner that reflects the relative value of each party's contribution to the combined profit. The reliability of the profit split method depends on the accuracy of data used to determine each party's relative contribution, including functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed. Comparability analysis under this method requires evaluation of (A) comparable intangible property — assessing whether the intangibles employed in the controlled transaction are similar in nature, scope, and economic value to those used in uncontrolled comparables — and (B) comparable circumstances, including market conditions, contractual terms, and the relative bargaining positions of the parties. The method further requires analysis of the data and assumptions underlying the profit allocation, ensuring that the profit division reflects an economically reasonable outcome consistent with how unrelated parties would allocate profits given similar facts. The arm's length range is determined from the results of multiple comparable uncontrolled transactions or from the application of alternative allocation keys (such as relative asset values, costs incurred, or headcount), and results falling within the range are treated as arm's length. Illustrative examples demonstrate application of the reliability and comparability criteria across varied factual settings.",
