      "text": "Section 1.482-3(f) provides special rules applicable when intangible property is transferred in connection with tangible goods transactions. Paragraph (f)(1) addresses the form of consideration, establishing that the arm's length amount for intangible property embedded in or accompanying a tangible goods transfer must be determined and documented separately where the intangible component has independent economic significance. Paragraph (f)(2) provides for periodic adjustments, reflecting the principle that where the value of transferred intangible property is subject to significant uncertainty at the time of transfer, the Commissioner may make periodic adjustments to ensure that the consideration charged over time remains consistent with the arm's length standard. These periodic adjustment provisions align with the commensurate-with-income standard under IRC § 482, which requires that the compensation for intangible property transfers reflect the actual economic returns generated by the intangible. The periodic adjustment mechanism is designed to prevent taxpayers from locking in a transfer price for high-value intangibles at a point of valuation uncertainty and then retaining supernormal profits as the intangible proves more valuable than anticipated. These rules apply in the context of tangible goods transactions where intangible property — such as embedded technology, know-how, or brand rights — is a material component of the overall transaction value, requiring separate arm's length analysis consistent with the methods prescribed under § 1.482-4.",
