      "title": "Arm's Length Range and Periodic Adjustments for Controlled Transfers of Intangible Property — § 1.482-3(i)",
      "text": "§ 1.482-3(i) sets out the general rule that the arm's length amount charged in a controlled transaction involving intangible property must be commensurate with the income attributable to the intangible. Under the general rule, the IRS may make periodic adjustments to reflect the actual profitability of the intangible over time, ensuring that the consideration paid in each taxable year reflects the income actually generated. Exceptions to the periodic adjustment rule are provided: (A) Transactions involving the same intangible — where a comparable uncontrolled transaction involving the same intangible exists that supports the price; (B) Transactions involving a comparable intangible — where a comparable uncontrolled transaction involving a comparable intangible supports a consistent pricing outcome; (C) Methods other than the comparable uncontrolled transaction method — where another best method provides a reliable measure of an arm's length result; (D) Extraordinary events — where significant changes in profitability result from extraordinary, unforeseeable events not within the control of the parties, which may excuse periodic adjustment; and (E) Five-year period — providing a safe harbor from periodic adjustments where the results fall within an arm's length range for five consecutive years. Additionally, paragraph (i)(3) addresses ownership of intangible property, clarifying which entity is treated as the owner of intangible property for purposes of allocating income under § 482. These provisions collectively implement the commensurate-with-income standard enacted by Congress in 1986 and align with OECD Guidelines Chapter VI concepts regarding the allocation of intangible-related returns to the entity that performs, assumes, and controls the relevant functions and risks associated with the intangible.",
