      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(i)",
      "title": "Transfer of Intangible Property in Connection with Tangible Goods Transactions — Allocation Rules and Ownership Identification",
      "text": "Under §1.482-3(i), where intangible property is transferred in connection with a sale or transfer of tangible goods between controlled parties, the Commissioner may make allocations to reflect an arm's length consideration for the intangible component. The provision addresses: (i) the general rule that consideration for embedded intangibles must be separately identified and priced on an arm's length basis; (ii) identification of the owner of the intangible, distinguishing between (A) legally protected intangible property — where ownership is determined by legal title or registration — and (B) intangible property that is not legally protected — where ownership is determined by the party that bears the risk and bears the cost of developing the intangible; (iii) allocations with respect to assistance provided to the owner, recognizing that a controlled party providing development or exploitation assistance to the legal owner may be entitled to arm's length compensation; and (iv) illustrative examples demonstrating application of these principles. Additionally, the provision establishes that (4) consideration for the intangible is not artificially limited by the structure of the tangible goods arrangement — i.e., the total arm's length return must reflect the full value of intangibles embedded in the transaction — and (5) lump sum payments may be used where a single payment appropriately captures the arm's length value of the intangible transferred, subject to the same arm's length standard as royalty-based arrangements. These rules align with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter VI principles on hard-to-value intangibles and the accurate delineation of transactions involving embedded intangibles.",
