      "text": "Section 1.482-3(i) establishes the general framework governing the determination of arm's length prices for controlled transfers of tangible property. Under the general rule, the arm's length price must be determined using the method that, under the facts and circumstances, provides the most reliable measure of an arm's length result consistent with the best method rule of § 1.482-1(c). The provision also sets forth exceptions to the general application of pricing methods for tangible property transfers, recognizing that certain circumstances may warrant deviation from standard method selection criteria. An illustrative example is provided to clarify the interaction between the general rule and its exceptions in practice. The provision serves as the gateway to § 1.482-5, which introduces the Comparable Profits Method (CPM) as an applicable method for evaluating controlled transactions involving tangible property. Collectively, § 1.482-3(i) anchors the analytical hierarchy for tangible goods pricing: the taxpayer must consider the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP), resale price, cost plus, comparable profits, and profit split methods, selecting the best method based on comparability, data quality, and reliability. Failure to apply the best method or to document the method selection process exposes the taxpayer to transfer pricing adjustments and potential penalties under § 6662(e) and § 6662(h). The provision aligns broadly with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, which similarly prescribes a most appropriate method standard for tangible goods transactions.",
