      "text": "Section 1.482-3(a) provides the general rule for evaluating whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction involving the transfer of tangible property represents an arm's length price. The arm's length price must be determined under one of the following methods: (1) the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method, described in paragraph (b); (2) the resale price method (RPM), described in paragraph (c); (3) the cost plus method (CPM), described in paragraph (d); (4) the comparable profits method (CPM), described in § 1.482-5; (5) the profit split method, described in § 1.482-6; or (6) unspecified methods, described in paragraph (e). The best method rule of § 1.482-1(c) must be applied to select the method that, under the facts and circumstances, provides the most reliable measure of an arm's length result. No method is considered inherently superior to another; the reliability of each method depends on the quality of the data and the degree of comparability between the controlled transaction and any uncontrolled comparables. This provision aligns with Chapter II of the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines, which similarly identifies traditional transaction methods and transactional profit methods as acceptable approaches for determining arm's length conditions for tangible property transfers.",
