      "title": "Comparable Uncontrolled Price Method — Determination of Arm's Length Result and Tested Party Selection",
      "text": "Under § 1.482-3(b), the arm's length result for transfers of tangible property is determined by reference to the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method. The CUP method evaluates whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction is arm's length by reference to the amount charged in a comparable uncontrolled transaction. The determination of the arm's length result requires identification of a tested party — generally the entity to which the transfer pricing method can be applied in the most reliable manner. The tested party is typically the less complex of the controlled taxpayers, the one that does not own valuable intangible property or bear significant entrepreneurial risk, and for which the most reliable comparables can be identified. The CUP method results in the most direct and reliable measure of an arm's length price when an identical or nearly identical uncontrolled transaction exists. Adjustments may be required to account for differences between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions that could materially affect price. This provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, Part A (the Comparable Uncontrolled Price method) and Chapter III guidance on comparability analysis.",
