      "title": "Comparable Profits Method — General Framework and Profit Level Indicators",
      "text": "Section 1.482-5 sets forth the comparable profits method (CPM) for evaluating whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction involving tangible property is arm's length. Under the CPM, the tested party's operating profit is compared to the operating profits earned by uncontrolled comparables performing similar functions, bearing similar risks, and employing similar assets. The tested party is generally the participant whose operating profit attributable to the controlled transaction can be verified using the most reliable data and requiring the fewest adjustments. The arm's length range is established using the results of multiple uncontrolled comparables, and if the tested party's reported profit falls outside this range, an allocation may be made to the median or another point within the range. Profit level indicators (PLIs) are ratios that measure relationships between operating profit and other financial variables, including: (1) the rate of return on capital employed, (2) the financial ratios of return on assets, and (3) operating margin (ratio of operating profit to sales). The most appropriate PLI depends on the facts and circumstances, including the nature of the tested party's business activities and the availability of reliable data. This method aligns with the OECD Guidelines' Transactional Net Margin Method (TNMM) under Chapter II, Part III, which similarly examines net profit margins relative to an appropriate base earned by a taxpayer from a controlled transaction.",
