      "text": "Under §1.482-3(i), profit level indicators used in applying the comparable profits method (CPM) to controlled transactions involving tangible property include: (i) Rate of return on capital employed — a ratio measuring operating profit relative to the capital assets deployed in the business activity; (ii) Financial ratios — standardized metrics such as gross margin, operating margin, or return on sales that relate profitability to revenue or cost bases; and (iii) Other profit level indicators — additional measures that reliably relate operating profit to an appropriate base reflecting the functions performed, assets employed, and risks assumed by the tested party. The selection among these indicators must produce the most reliable measure of an arm's length result, taking into account the nature of the business operations, the availability of comparable data, and the degree to which the indicator reflects the value contributed by the tested party. These profit level indicators align with the transactional net margin method (TNMM) under OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, Part III, which similarly uses net profit indicators relative to an appropriate base (costs, sales, or assets) to assess arm's length outcomes.",
