      "id": "irc482-3c",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(c)",
      "title": "Resale Price Method — Comparability and Reliability Considerations",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(c) addresses the comparability and reliability considerations applicable to the resale price method (RPM) for determining arm's length prices in controlled transactions involving transfers of tangible property. Under the general rule, the reliability of the RPM as a transfer pricing method depends on the degree of comparability between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions, with particular emphasis on functional similarity between the reseller in the controlled transaction and resellers in uncontrolled transactions. Comparability under the RPM is assessed by examining the functions performed, risks assumed, and contractual terms of the reseller, as well as the degree of similarity in the property or services involved. Because the RPM focuses on the reseller's gross profit margin rather than the characteristics of the product itself, product similarity is less critical than functional comparability. Differences in accounting practices that affect gross margins must be identified and adjusted. The method is most reliable when the reseller adds minimal value to the product prior to resale, performs similar functions, and assumes comparable risks relative to uncontrolled resellers. Where significant differences exist between the controlled and uncontrolled transactions, adjustments must be made to enhance comparability, and the reliability of the method diminishes to the extent such differences cannot be reliably quantified and adjusted. This provision aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the resale price method, which similarly emphasizes functional comparability over product comparability and requires attention to accounting consistency when applying gross margin benchmarks.",
