      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(i)",
      "title": "Comparable Profits Method — Comparability and Data Requirements for Tested Party Analysis",
      "text": "Under the comparable profits method (CPM) as applied to controlled transfers of tangible property, comparability between the tested party and uncontrolled comparables must be assessed across multiple dimensions. (i) In general: The CPM evaluates whether the operating profit earned by the tested party reflects arm's length conditions by reference to profit level indicators (PLIs) earned by uncontrolled taxpayers engaged in similar activities. (ii) Functional, risk and resource comparability: The most important comparability factors under the CPM are the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed by the tested party relative to uncontrolled comparables. Differences in these factors directly affect the reliability of the PLI comparison and may require adjustments or render a potential comparable unreliable. (iii) Other comparability factors: Additional factors affecting comparability include product similarity, contractual terms, economic conditions, and business strategies. While product similarity is less critical under the CPM than under transactional methods, significant differences in market conditions or business cycles may affect the reliability of the comparison. (iv) Adjustments for differences between tested party and the uncontrolled taxpayers: If there are material differences between the tested party and uncontrolled comparables that affect the PLI, adjustments must be made to improve comparability, provided that the adjustments are likely to increase the reliability of the results and can be made with reasonable accuracy. (3) Data and assumptions: The results of a CPM analysis depend on the data available and the assumptions made. The reliability of the analysis is enhanced when data from multiple years is used, when the accounting methods of the tested party and comparables are consistent, and when the comparables selected reflect similar economic and market conditions.",
