      "title": "Tangible Property Transfer Pricing — General Rules, Accounting Consistency, and Activity Allocations",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(i) establishes overarching principles governing the determination of arm's length prices for transfers of tangible property between controlled taxpayers. Sub-paragraph (i) sets out the general framework requiring that the arm's length price be determined using the most reliable method among those specified under § 1.482-3, including the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP), resale price, cost plus, comparable profits, and profit split methods. Sub-paragraph (ii) requires consistency in accounting when applying any transfer pricing method: the same accounting classifications, cost allocation approaches, and financial statement conventions must be applied consistently across the tested party and any comparables used, so that differences in accounting treatment do not distort method results or comparability analysis. Where accounting inconsistencies exist between the controlled transaction and uncontrolled comparables, appropriate adjustments must be made. Sub-paragraph (iii) addresses allocations between the relevant business activity under examination and other activities of the taxpayer: where a taxpayer's operations span multiple functions or product lines, costs, revenues, assets, and risks attributable specifically to the controlled transaction must be properly segregated from those relating to other activities. This ensures that the financial data used to apply a transfer pricing method accurately reflects only the economics of the transaction under review, consistent with OECD Guidelines Chapter I guidance on delineating the actual transaction and Chapter II guidance on applying transactional methods to correctly scoped data.",
