      "text": "Section 1.482-3(e) provides examples illustrating the application of the methods described in § 1.482-3 for determining arm's length prices in controlled transactions involving transfers of tangible property. These examples demonstrate the operation of the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method, resale price method, cost plus method, comparable profits method, and profit split method in various fact patterns. The subsection cross-references § 1.482-6, which governs the profit split method, signaling that the profit split method is among the methods available for evaluating the arm's length nature of controlled transfers of tangible property where other methods cannot be reliably applied. The examples serve as interpretive guidance for taxpayers and examiners in applying the best method rule under § 1.482-1(c), which requires selection of the method that provides the most reliable measure of an arm's length result. Taken together, § 1.482-3(e) and the cross-reference to § 1.482-6 affirm that no single method is automatically preferred for tangible property transactions, and that the profit split method remains a viable option when transactional methods or one-sided profit-based methods are insufficiently reliable due to, for example, each party contributing significant valuable intangibles or where highly integrated operations make separate entity analysis impractical. This aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II (traditional transaction methods) and Chapter III (transactional profit methods), which similarly recognize the residual profit split as an appropriate method under comparable circumstances.",
