      "title": "Transfer Pricing Methods for Tangible Property — General Rule",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(a) provides that the arm's length amount charged in a controlled transaction involving the transfer of tangible property must be determined under one of the following methods: (1) the comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method described in paragraph (b); (2) the resale price method described in paragraph (c); (3) the cost plus method described in paragraph (d); (4) the comparable profits method (CPM) described in § 1.482-5; (5) the profit split method described in § 1.482-6; or (6) an unspecified method described in paragraph (e). The method that, under the facts and circumstances, provides the most reliable measure of an arm's length result must be selected, consistent with the best method rule of § 1.482-1(c). No method is presumed to be the most reliable measure of an arm's length result, and the taxpayer is not required to use the method yielding the highest or lowest price. The reliability of a given method depends on the degree of comparability between the controlled transaction and uncontrolled comparables, the quality of data and assumptions used in the analysis, and whether adjustments can account for material differences.",
