      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(b)",
      "title": "Arm's Length Standard — Controlled Taxpayer's Appropriate Share of Profits and Losses",
      "text": "§ 1.482-3(b) provides that the arm's length result for a controlled transaction involving tangible property must reflect the appropriate share of profits and losses that would be realized by uncontrolled taxpayers engaged in the same or similar transactions under the same or similar circumstances. The allocation of profits and losses between controlled parties must reflect the economic substance of the arrangement, taking into account the functions performed, risks assumed, and assets employed by each party. Where a controlled taxpayer bears a risk that an uncontrolled taxpayer would not bear under comparable circumstances, or where the allocation of profit does not correspond to the allocation of risk and the performance of functions, the Commissioner may make allocations to reflect an arm's length result. The appropriate share of profits is determined by reference to what uncontrolled parties would have agreed to in comparable transactions, consistent with the best method rule under § 1.482-1(c). This principle aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter I (the arm's length principle) and Chapter II (transfer pricing methods), particularly in relation to the functional analysis required to determine how residual and routine profits should be allocated among controlled participants.",
