      "id": "irc482-3c",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(c)",
      "title": "Transfer Pricing for Tangible Property: Resale Price Method",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(c) provides the resale price method (RPM) for determining the arm's length price for transfers of tangible property. Under the RPM, the arm's length price is determined by subtracting an appropriate gross profit margin from the applicable resale price. The appropriate gross profit margin is derived from comparable uncontrolled transactions in which the reseller performs similar functions, bears similar risks, and uses similar assets. The resale price is the price at which property purchased in a controlled transaction is resold in an uncontrolled transaction. The gross profit margin earned in comparable uncontrolled transactions is used to establish the arm's length gross profit margin, which is then subtracted from the resale price to determine the arm's length purchase price. Comparability under the RPM is particularly sensitive to functional comparability, including the nature and extent of value-added activities performed by the reseller, contractual terms, risks assumed, and economic conditions. Product similarity is less critical than under the comparable uncontrolled price method, provided that the functions performed and risks borne are sufficiently comparable. The method aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II guidance on the resale price method as a one-sided transactional method appropriate where the reseller does not add significant value to the tangible property prior to resale.",
