      "id": "irc482-3i",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-3(i)",
      "title": "Profit Split Methods for Controlled Transfers of Tangible Property — Comparability, Reliability, and Residual Profit Split",
      "text": "Section 1.482-3(i) governs the application of profit split methods to controlled transactions involving transfers of tangible property. Under the general rule, a profit split method evaluates whether the allocation of combined operating profit or loss attributable to one or more controlled transactions is arm's length by reference to the relative value of each controlled taxpayer's contribution to that combined profit or loss. Comparability and reliability considerations require that the combined profits be derived from the same business activity and that the allocation reflect economically significant contributions. Comparability analysis must assess the functions performed, risks assumed, and resources employed by each party; adjustments must be made for material differences between controlled and uncontrolled taxpayers that affect the reliability of the profit split. The data and assumptions underlying the method must be sufficiently complete and accurate to permit a reliable measure of the arm's length result. Other factors affecting reliability include the extent to which the profit split is consistent with independent evidence of the relative value of each party's contributions, such as market benchmarks or internal financial data. The residual profit split further allocates combined profit in two steps: first, routine returns are assigned to each party for its non-unique contributions based on market benchmarks; second, any residual profit (typically attributable to valuable intangibles or unique contributions) is allocated among the controlled parties based on the relative value of their contributions to that residual, using external market data or internal measures of intangible value where available. This method aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, Part III on profit split methods, particularly the residual profit split approach described therein.",
