      "text": "Section 1.482-3(i) establishes the profit split method as an applicable method for determining arm's length consideration in controlled transactions involving transfers of tangible property. The method proceeds in two stages: (i)(A) allocate income to routine contributions by each controlled party, benchmarked against returns earned by comparable uncontrolled parties performing similar functions and bearing similar risks; and (i)(B) allocate residual profit (or loss) remaining after routine returns are assigned, typically based on the relative value of non-routine contributions such as intangibles or unique functions. Comparability and reliability considerations under (ii) require that: (A) the method be applied with sufficient comparability to uncontrolled transactions or parties; (B) the comparability analysis account for functions performed, risks assumed, and contractual terms; (C) data and assumptions used in the profit split calculation be reliable and verifiable; and (D) other factors affecting reliability, including the availability of data on comparable uncontrolled parties and the consistency of accounting practices, be evaluated. The profit split method is most reliable when both parties make valuable, unique contributions to the controlled transaction, when integrated operations make separate entity analysis difficult, or when no reliable comparable uncontrolled transactions are available for one or both parties. An illustrative example is provided to demonstrate proper application of the two-step allocation procedure. This method broadly aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, Part III (profit split method), including the contribution and residual profit split variants.",
