      "text": "Section 1.482-4 governs methods for determining arm's length consideration in controlled transfers of intangible property. The primary prescribed method is the Comparable Uncontrolled Transaction (CUT) method, which evaluates the transfer by reference to uncontrolled transactions involving comparable intangible property under comparable circumstances. Comparability requires assessment of the intangible's nature, terms of transfer, economic conditions, and the functions and risks of the parties. Unspecified methods may also be used where no specified method reliably measures the arm's length result. Special rules address: (1) form of consideration — the IRS may evaluate any form of payment; (2) periodic adjustments — the Commissioner may adjust consideration in subsequent years if actual results diverge materially from projections, subject to exceptions for transactions involving the same or comparable intangible, use of methods other than CUT, extraordinary events, and a five-year safe period; (3) ownership — allocations are made based on legal ownership and, for unprotected intangibles, on the party that bears development risk and cost; (4) consideration not artificially limited — arm's length consideration must reflect the full value of the intangible and may not be contractually capped below that value; and (5) lump sum payments — subject to periodic adjustment rules unless exceptions apply. Section 1.482-5 provides the Comparable Profits Method (CPM), testing a party's operating profit against uncontrolled comparables using profit level indicators such as return on capital employed and financial ratios. Section 1.482-6 provides the Profit Split Method, allocating combined operating profit between controlled parties based on either a comparable profit split or a residual profit split, the latter first allocating routine returns then splitting residual profit attributable to valuable intangibles.",
