      "id": "irc482-5",
      "frameworkId": "irc-482",
      "citation": "§ 1.482-5",
      "title": "Comparable Profits Method (CPM) — Determination of Arm's Length Result and Profit Level Indicators",
      "text": "Section 1.482-5 establishes the Comparable Profits Method (CPM) for evaluating whether the amount charged in a controlled transaction is arm's length. Under CPM, the arm's length result is determined by reference to objective measures of profitability (profit level indicators, or PLIs) derived from uncontrolled taxpayers that engage in similar business activities under similar circumstances. The tested party is generally the participant in the controlled transaction whose operating profit attributable to the controlled transactions can be verified using the most reliable data and requires the fewest and most reliable adjustments; typically this is the least complex entity. Adjustments to the tested party's results may be required to improve comparability. The arm's length range is established from the PLIs of comparable uncontrolled companies. Permissible PLIs include: (i) rate of return on capital employed, measuring the ratio of operating profit to operating assets; (ii) financial ratios such as ratio of operating profit to sales or to total costs; and (iii) other profit level indicators that provide reliable measures of profitability. Comparability under CPM requires functional, risk, and resource comparability between the tested party and uncontrolled comparables, with adjustments for material differences. Data and assumptions must be applied consistently in accounting treatment, and allocations must be made between the relevant business activity and other activities of the tested party. Section 1.482-6 addresses the Profit Split Method, under which the combined operating profit or loss from a controlled transaction is allocated between the controlled taxpayers in proportion to each party's relative contribution, consistent with what uncontrolled parties would have agreed. Two variants are recognized: the Comparable Profit Split, which relies on external benchmarks, and the Residual Profit Split, which first allocates income to routine contributions using market returns and then allocates the residual profit (attributable to non-routine, typically intangible contributions) based on relative value of each party's non-routine contributions.",
