      "title": "Profit Split Method — Comparable and Residual Profit Split Approaches Under §1.482-6",
      "text": "Section 1.482-6 provides the profit split method as a means to evaluate whether the allocation of combined profit or loss attributable to one or more controlled transactions is arm's length. Under §1.482-6(b), each controlled participant must receive an appropriate share of profits and losses based on the relative value of its contributions. §1.482-6(c) sets out two primary applications: (1) the comparable profit split, which derives the arm's length split by reference to the division of operating profit observed among uncontrolled taxpayers engaged in similar activities, with reliability dependent on functional comparability, adjustment feasibility, and data quality; and (2) the residual profit split, which proceeds in two steps — first allocating income sufficient to provide each participant a market return on routine contributions (using external benchmarks analogous to those used under the comparable profits method), then allocating any residual profit or loss based on the relative value of non-routine contributions, typically unique and valuable intangibles. Comparability under both variants requires analysis of functions performed, risks assumed, resources employed, and contractual terms. Adjustments must be made for material differences between controlled and uncontrolled arrangements. The reliability of the method is further affected by the quality and consistency of data and the assumptions underlying the profit allocation. The residual profit split is particularly appropriate where both parties to the controlled transaction contribute valuable, non-routine intangibles such that no reliable one-sided method can be applied. This method aligns with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines Chapter II, Part III (profit split method) and Chapter VI (intangibles), particularly where unique contributions preclude the identification of adequate comparable uncontrolled transactions.",
